# C: the view descriptor's integer range bounds are exact integers, not doubles

PORT TO THE STANDARD (Glenn 2026-10-18: \"We do all features across all languages.\"). The standard is docs/SPEC-TABLES.md and docs/USAGE.md as they stand on this base after the forward merge (c-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f, which brings mas-bandwidth/schema sprint/schema-go at d808c3f54); schema-go's behaviour there is the oracle. This card is one feature of the C port and nothing else.

THE TASK. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md (read) §8.1 and §4: the C field descriptor carries `double range_min; double range_max;` with the comment \"int64 ranges beyond 2^53 lose precision here\", so a declared `max = 9007199254740993` reads back as 9007199254740992. Go carries exact columns: RangeIntMin/RangeIntMax (int64) and RangeUintMax (uint64) beside the float pair, and an int128 field carries no int64 pair. Add the same exact columns to the C descriptor, filled from the declaration's exact integers, keep the double pair for float ranges, and make every runtime reader of an integer range (the JSON clamp, the view's generic walk) read the exact column. Files: internal/codegen/ctable/view.go,internal/codegen/ctable/ctable.go, internal/codegen/ctable/json.go, the generated trees.

THE ORACLE. schema-go at d808c3f54 is the answer key, and it is on this base after the forward merge: internal/codegen/gotable/exactrange_test.go TestGoExactIntegerRangeBounds (the `fixed table Big` with n, m, u and w, and the clamp it asserts at each exact edge). Every expected answer the new test asserts is the one that Go test pins for the same schema text and the same input bytes, transcribed into the C probe with a comment citing the Go file and the case; never a value this port computed for itself. Read the Go emitter beside it (internal/codegen/gotable, named in the citation) for the rule's edges before writing the C one.

THE GAP AT BASE. grep -n 'lose precision' internal/codegen/ctable/view.go internal/codegen/ctable/ctable.go shows the double-only range columns at BASE.

