# C: the view descriptor says UnboundedContainer explicitly

PORT TO THE STANDARD (Glenn 2026-10-18: \"We do all features across all languages.\"). The standard is docs/SPEC-TABLES.md and docs/USAGE.md as they stand on this base after the forward merge (c-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f, which brings mas-bandwidth/schema sprint/schema-go at d808c3f54); schema-go's behaviour there is the oracle. This card is one feature of the C port and nothing else.

THE TASK. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md (read) §8.1 and §2.9: a reflection reader must tell \"no bound\" from a bound of 2^31 - 1, so the field descriptor carries an explicit unbounded_container column: true with array_bound 0 for a map and a []T, false with the bound for a [..N]T. The C descriptor has no such column. Add it (C: unbounded_container; C++: the same member name the C++ descriptor's snake_case uses), fill it for every map and unbounded array, and make the generic view walk read it rather than infer from array_bound. Text-list rows (unbounded text, §2.13.1) arrive with their own cards. Files: internal/codegen/ctable/view.go,internal/codegen/ctable/ctable.go, the generated trees.

THE ORACLE. schema-go at d808c3f54 is the answer key, and it is on this base after the forward merge: internal/codegen/gotable/unboundeddescriptor_test.go TestGoUnboundedDescriptorIsExplicit (its map, []int32 and [..4]int32 rows). Every expected answer the new test asserts is the one that Go test pins for the same schema text and the same input bytes, transcribed into the C probe with a comment citing the Go file and the case; never a value this port computed for itself. Read the Go emitter beside it (internal/codegen/gotable, named in the citation) for the rule's edges before writing the C one.

THE GAP AT BASE. grep -n 'unbounded_container\\|UnboundedContainer' internal/codegen/ctable/view.go internal/codegen/ctable/ctable.go prints nothing at BASE.

