# C: a fixed-form count or length past its bound refuses count_over_bound / length_over_bound

PORT TO THE STANDARD (Glenn 2026-10-18: \"We do all features across all languages.\"). The standard is docs/SPEC-TABLES.md and docs/USAGE.md as they stand on this base after the forward merge (the forward merge, which brings mas-bandwidth/schema sprint/schema-go at d808c3f54); schema-go's behaviour there is the oracle. This card is one feature of the C port and nothing else.

THE TASK. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md (read) §3.4 and docs/FIXED-FORM-ALGORITHM.md (read) (4edb98c8a): a fixed-form record whose array count or text length is past the PLAN's bound is REFUSED by name, count_over_bound or length_over_bound, nothing decoded and no counter moved; it is no longer clamped. The C fixed runtime clamps it today. Add the two names to the fixed form's reason enum (after text_ill_formed, in Go's TableFixedReason order), make the plan read refuse with them, and move the port's existing clamp-ends tests to the refusal (each moved assertion cites the Go test row it now matches). Files: internal/codegen/ctable/fixedform.go, internal/codegen/ctable/fixedruntime.go, the generated trees.

THE ORACLE. schema-go at d808c3f54 is the answer key, and it is on this base after the forward merge: internal/codegen/gotable/fixedbound_test.go (every row) and the edited rows of internal/codegen/gotable/fixedversioning_count_clamp_ends_test.go. Every expected answer the new test asserts is the one that Go test pins for the same schema text and the same input bytes, transcribed into the C probe with a comment citing the Go file and the case; never a value this port computed for itself. Read the Go emitter beside it (internal/codegen/gotable, named in the citation) for the rule's edges before writing the C one.

THE GAP AT BASE. grep -n 'count_over_bound\\|length_over_bound\\|COUNT_OVER_BOUND' internal/codegen/ctable/*.go prints nothing at BASE, and cT06CountClampHigh (internal/codegen/ctable/fixedroadmap_t06_test.go) still asserts a clamp.

Do not edit docs/SPEC-TABLES.md or docs/USAGE.md: where this card makes a backend-status sentence there stale (\"Go is first and the only target\", \"every other target refuses\"), quote the sentence and the one-line replacement in the pull request as a proposed diff for the spec helper.

THE C++ TWIN ALREADY WROTE THE C HALF (coordinator, 2026-10-19). A read of cpp-fixed-count-and-length-over-bound-refuse-by-name found its head 88e7493003dd (on sprint/schema-cpp) also changed the C side; use `git diff origin/sprint/schema-cpp...88e7493003dd -- internal/codegen/ctable internal/tablenames tools/fixedtwin generated/bench/paired/c` in a clone of mas-bandwidth/schema as the worked reference (fetch that commit by sha). It: (1) in internal/codegen/ctable/fixedruntime.go adds SCHEMA_TABLE_COUNT_OVER_BOUND = 21 and SCHEMA_TABLE_LENGTH_OVER_BOUND = 22 after SCHEMA_TABLE_TEXT_ILL_FORMED = 20 in the reason enum, and a pre-scan table_fixed_refuse_scan that walks the plan under its guards (the partition split, the pooled guard chain, guard2) and refuses a kTableFixedCount past p->size as count_over_bound and a kTableFixedText length past p->size/unit (2 for wide) as length_over_bound before any destination byte is written; (2) in internal/tablenames/c.go names table_fixed_refuse_scan; (3) in tools/fixedtwin/canon.go maps the two new identifiers to count_over_bound/length_over_bound, widens reCReasons to end at `length_over_bound = 22`, and adds TableFixedRefuseScan to the bool-return rewrites; (4) regenerates generated/bench/paired/c/BenchTable.h and FixedTableTable.h. Port the same change onto this card's BASE (sprint/schema-c), wire the scan into the C fixed loader so the load refuses with it, and regenerate every C tree the change moves; the Go oracle stays the answer key for every assertion.

WHERE IT LANDS: this card lands on sprint/schema-c only; it reaches sprint/schema-cpp through the next schema-c to schema-cpp forward merge, never by a C edit on the C++ card.
