# C: the plain binary Load is the strict read, ReadStrict exists, and the tolerant read has a name of its own

PORT TO THE STANDARD (Glenn 2026-10-18: \"We do all features across all languages.\"). The standard is docs/SPEC-TABLES.md and docs/USAGE.md as they stand on this base after the forward merge (c-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f, which brings mas-bandwidth/schema sprint/schema-go at d808c3f54); schema-go's behaviour there is the oracle. This card is one feature of the C port and nothing else.

THE TASK. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md (read) §6.5, \"THE PLAIN NAME IS THE STRICT READ; THE TOLERANT READ HAS A NAME OF ITS OWN\", and §11's ruling that the default read refuses at every meaning boundary. In the C port the plain binary entry points are tolerant today: the value Load of a fixed-class root, the region Load of a variable root and LoadBuilder all answer success with clamped, kind_mismatch or malformed counted. Make each plain name refuse whatever the strict read refuses (malformed, clamped, kind-mismatched, unknown form, wire-damaged; non_finite and blob_shared arrive with their own cards) with the refusal value its signature already has (false for the value Load and LoadBuilder, a NULL root for the region Load), the supplied report overwritten with this read's report and its reason set to the TableRefuseReason value; on a refusal the value Load resets the value to its declared defaults and the region Load hands back no root. Add the tolerant twins under the claimed names LoadTolerant and LoadBuilderTolerant (C spells each name in the port's own snake_case form (`<root>_load`, `<root>_load_tolerant`, the claimed-suffix row of docs/SPEC-TABLES.md (read) §11 spelled as `<name>_fixed_save` is spelled)), each with exactly the signature and the answers the plain name had at BASE, and add ReadStrict for each variable root (one call, its own builder, the root or a refusal with one reason). unknown and widened alone never refuse. Every caller in the repo that relied on the tolerant answer (test/c-tables/**,test/conformance/c/**,bench/tables/c/**) moves to the Tolerant name, so no existing test changes meaning. Files: internal/codegen/ctable/wire_read.go,internal/codegen/ctable/ctable.go,internal/codegen/ctable/graph.go, internal/tablenames/c.go, the generated trees.

THE ORACLE. schema-go at d808c3f54 is the answer key, and it is on this base after the forward merge: internal/codegen/gotable/strictdefault_test.go TestGoDefaultReadIsStrict (its binary rows: a clamped value, a kind mismatch, a truncated body, each refused by the plain Load and recovered by LoadTolerant with the same report). Every expected answer the new test asserts is the one that Go test pins for the same schema text and the same input bytes, transcribed into the C probe with a comment citing the Go file and the case; never a value this port computed for itself. Read the Go emitter beside it (internal/codegen/gotable, named in the citation) for the rule's edges before writing the C one.

THE GAP AT BASE. grep -n 'LoadTolerant\\|load_tolerant\\|ReadStrict\\|read_strict' internal/codegen/ctable/wire_read.go internal/codegen/ctable/ctable.go internal/codegen/ctable/graph.go prints nothing at BASE.

Do not edit docs/SPEC-TABLES.md or docs/USAGE.md: where this card makes a backend-status sentence there stale (\"Go is first and the only target\", \"every other target refuses\"), quote the sentence and the one-line replacement in the pull request as a proposed diff for the spec helper.

