# C++: SaveText and LoadText, canonical base64 of the binary wire, LoadText strict

PORT TO THE STANDARD (Glenn 2026-10-18: \"We do all features across all languages.\"). The standard is docs/SPEC-TABLES.md and docs/USAGE.md as they stand on this base after the forward merge (cpp-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f, which brings mas-bandwidth/schema sprint/schema-go at d808c3f54); schema-go's behaviour there is the oracle. This card is one feature of the C++ port and nothing else.

THE TASK. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md (read) §16.8: EVERY TABLE HAS BOTH text carriers, and the C++ port has the JSON one only. Emit SaveText and LoadText for every root (C++ spells each name as Go does (`<Name>Load`, `<Name>LoadTolerant`), the reference spelling of docs/SPEC-TABLES.md (read) §11): SaveText writes the canonical base64 (standard alphabet, padded, pad bits zero, no whitespace) of exactly the bytes Save writes; LoadText decodes and reads with the plain strict Load (a fixed root: a value filled by Load; a variable root: its own builder and the locked root, as ReadStrict does), refusing whenever the report holds an error. A text that, with its CR and LF dropped, is padded standard base64 that decodes but is not the canonical encoding of those bytes refuses base64_not_canonical; every other failure (a byte outside the alphabet and `=`, a missing, extra or misplaced `=`, a text that does not decode) refuses base64_damaged; a decoded wire the binary read refuses is that read's own refusal. report is optional; a supplied one receives this read's report. No allocation on the fixed path beyond what Load has; claim both suffixes in internal/tablenames/cpp.go. Files: internal/codegen/cpptable/text.go,internal/codegen/cpptable/cpptable.go, internal/tablenames/cpp.go, the generated trees.

THE ORACLE. schema-go at d808c3f54 is the answer key, and it is on this base after the forward merge: internal/codegen/gotable/base64canonical_test.go TestGoLoadTextRefusesNonCanonicalBase64 (its pad-bit, inner `=`, whitespace, CR/LF and wrong-padding rows and their names) and the SaveText/LoadText round trips of internal/codegen/gotable/unboundeddescriptor_test.go. Every expected answer the new test asserts is the one that Go test pins for the same schema text and the same input bytes, transcribed into the C++ probe with a comment citing the Go file and the case; never a value this port computed for itself. Read the Go emitter beside it (internal/codegen/gotable, named in the citation) for the rule's edges before writing the C++ one.

THE GAP AT BASE. grep -n 'SaveText\\|save_text\\|LoadText\\|load_text' internal/codegen/cpptable/*.go prints nothing at BASE.

Do not edit docs/SPEC-TABLES.md or docs/USAGE.md: where this card makes a backend-status sentence there stale (\"Go is first and the only target\", \"every other target refuses\"), quote the sentence and the one-line replacement in the pull request as a proposed diff for the spec helper. Bytes fields inside JSON (schema-go-json-base64-canonical) are still WORKING on schema-go and are NOT this card.

DONE-WHEN: go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./compiler/ -run 'TestCPPLoadTextRefusesNonCanonicalBase64' fails at BASE (its failing assertion, kept from STEP 2) and passes at head; LoadText(SaveText(v)) is byte for byte Save(v) for every root of the bench corpus unit the probe generates; go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/cpptable/ ./compiler/ ./internal/ci/, then make generated-current and make build/schema_test_tables && ./build/schema_test_tables are green or their only reds are named as present at BASE; the grep in THE GAP AT BASE no longer shows the gap at head.

