# C++: a table's `forms` declaration is honoured: the port emits only the forms a table declares

PORT TO THE STANDARD (Glenn 2026-10-18: \"We do all features across all languages.\"). The standard is docs/SPEC-TABLES.md and docs/USAGE.md as they stand on this base after the forward merge (cpp-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f, which brings mas-bandwidth/schema sprint/schema-go at d808c3f54); schema-go's behaviour there is the oracle. This card is one feature of the C++ port and nothing else.

THE TASK. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md (read) §2.12: a table may declare the forms it carries, and the generator emits exactly those surfaces (and the surface intersection §8.8 states). compiler/generate.go (read) refuses a unit that declares forms for every target but go (the firstFormsTable branch, line 111 after the forward merge). Carry it for \"cpp\": gate each surface the C++ emitter writes (wire, message, fixed, json, cook, block, view) on the table's declared forms as Go's surface.go does, then take \"cpp\" out of that branch. Files: internal/codegen/cpptable/cpptable.go, internal/codegen/cpptable/view.go, compiler/generate.go (read), the generated trees.

THE ORACLE. schema-go at d808c3f54 is the answer key, and it is on this base after the forward merge: internal/codegen/gotable/declaredforms_test.go (TestGoTableEmitsOnlyItsDeclaredForms, TestSurfaceIntersectsDeclaredForms). Every expected answer the new test asserts is the one that Go test pins for the same schema text and the same input bytes, transcribed into the C++ probe with a comment citing the Go file and the case; never a value this port computed for itself. Read the Go emitter beside it (internal/codegen/gotable, named in the citation) for the rule's edges before writing the C++ one.

THE GAP AT BASE. compiler/generate.go line 111 refuses a unit declaring forms for \"cpp\".

