# Elixir: save_text/load_text, the canonical base64 text carrier, for both classes

SOURCE (Glenn, 2026-10-18: \"We do all features across all languages\"). The standard is docs/SPEC-TABLES.md at d808c3f54 (schema-go), which arrives on this base with elixir-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f; schema-go's behaviour there is the oracle. Never write spec text: a section that is silent or stale is reported, not edited. Family base64-canonical and the §16.8 carrier feature: docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §16.8 (EVERY TABLE HAS BOTH carriers; the base64 is CANONICAL; base64_not_canonical vs base64_damaged; LoadText IS THE STRICT READ); oracle internal/codegen/gotable/base64canonical_test.go TestGoLoadTextRefusesNonCanonicalBase64. The bytes-inside-JSON half is schema-go-json-base64-canonical, still working on schema-go: not this card.

RED AT BASE (BASE is the base after elixir-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f landed). the Elixir port has no base64 carrier at all: `grep -c 'save_text\\|load_text' internal/codegen/elixirtable/*.go` counts 0 in every emitter.

THE TASK. Emit, from a new internal/codegen/elixirtable/textcarrier.go called from elixirtable.go's Generate, `save_text` and `load_text` for every root of both classes (§16.8's shape follows the class's binary pair: the variable root's `save_text(table, value)` encodes `save`'s bytes and `load_text(table, text)` decodes and runs the STRICT `load/2`; a fixed root's `<t>_save_text/1` and `<t>_load_text/1` over `<t>_fixed_save` and the strict `<t>_fixed_load`). The read refuses by name: `:base64_not_canonical` when dropping CR and LF leaves padded standard base64 that decodes but the text is not exactly its canonical encoding (a pad bit set, a CR or LF present), `:base64_damaged` for every other failure (a byte outside the alphabet and `=`, a space, a missing, extra or misplaced `=`), and the binary reader's own refusal for a decoded wire it refuses. `save_text` refuses what save refuses. Test TestElixirLoadTextRefusesNonCanonicalBase64 in the new textcarrier_test.go, rows from the Go test: the fuzz repro `AQAAAAAAAAAAAB==` on tables/examples WeaponConfig, a newline mid-text, an appended CRLF, a leading `!`, an embedded space, a missing and an extra `=`, each with the reason Go names; a canonical save_text that loads clean and re-encodes to the same text; the same over a variable root (Guarded's Patrol); and save_text's text equal to Go's base64 of the engine's bytes for the same value. Expected answers come from schema-go at d808c3f54 and are never typed from memory: bytes, texts and refusal reasons are read from the engine packages internal/tablewire, internal/tabletext and internal/tablepack inside the Go test (the way internal/codegen/elixirtable/textdifferential_test.go (read) uses them), and where the engine has no surface for the answer the expected value is the one the named Go test pins, cited by file and line in a comment. The Elixir probe is generated into t.TempDir() and run under SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1 (skip by name without the toolchain, fail by name with it), as keyed_test.go does; a Go test that only reads generator output is a unit test, one that runs elixir is a functional test reached from a make target in make/elixir.mk that fails on any SKIP and is listed in test-elixir.

DONE-WHEN: SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1 go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/elixirtable -run '^TestElixirLoadTextRefusesNonCanonicalBase64$' -v shows RUN and --- PASS, and the same command at BASE prints `testing: warning: no tests to run` (the test is new) while the gap named under RED AT BASE holds there; make tables-elixir-text-carrier (new, in test-elixir) is green; the committed generated Elixir is current (make test-elixir's generated-current and format checks green); make roadmap-check is clean.

