# Elixir: carry `was` on enum variants, packet-union arms and type fields

SOURCE (Glenn, 2026-10-18: \"We do all features across all languages\"). The standard is docs/SPEC-TABLES.md at d808c3f54 (schema-go), which arrives on this base with elixir-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f; schema-go's behaviour there is the oracle. Never write spec text: a section that is silent or stale is reported, not edited. Feature: identity by name and `was` (docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §5). Oracle compiler/wasrows_test.go TestWasRowsCarriers and the Go leg's identity hashing of a renamed variant, arm and field.

RED AT BASE (BASE is the base after elixir-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f landed). compiler/target_elixir.go:47-50 calls refuseWasRows (compiler/wasrows.go) for elixir unless every `was` sits on a table-union arm (elixirCarriesTableArmWasOnly, target_elixir.go:102-125): a `was` on an enum variant, a packet-union arm or a type's field refuses the unit.

THE TASK. Carry the three rows: an enum variant's `was` reads the old variant name's id as the new variant, a packet union arm's `was` dispatches the old arm, a type field's `was` reads the old field, on the variable wire, the message form and JSON as Go does; then register elixir as a was-row carrier (the append registration compiler/wasrows.go (read) reads) and delete elixirCarriesTableArmWasOnly. Test TestElixirWasRowsCarried in the new wasrows_test.go: a unit renaming one of each, an older writer's bytes (from the engine over the old unit) read by the new Elixir build equal to the engine's read; TestWasRowsCarriers lists elixir. Expected answers come from schema-go at d808c3f54 and are never typed from memory: bytes, texts and refusal reasons are read from the engine packages internal/tablewire, internal/tabletext and internal/tablepack inside the Go test (the way internal/codegen/elixirtable/textdifferential_test.go (read) uses them), and where the engine has no surface for the answer the expected value is the one the named Go test pins, cited by file and line in a comment. The Elixir probe is generated into t.TempDir() and run under SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1 (skip by name without the toolchain, fail by name with it), as keyed_test.go does; a Go test that only reads generator output is a unit test, one that runs elixir is a functional test reached from a make target in make/elixir.mk that fails on any SKIP and is listed in test-elixir.

