# Elixir: the descriptor says an unbounded container explicitly

SOURCE (Glenn, 2026-10-18: \"We do all features across all languages\"). The standard is docs/SPEC-TABLES.md at d808c3f54 (schema-go), which arrives on this base with elixir-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f; schema-go's behaviour there is the oracle. Never write spec text: a section that is silent or stale is reported, not edited. Family bounds, the descriptor: docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §8.1 (the UnboundedContainer column, array_bound 0 beside it) and §2.9; oracle internal/codegen/gotable/unboundeddescriptor_test.go TestGoUnboundedDescriptorIsExplicit.

RED AT BASE (BASE is the base after elixir-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f landed). the descriptors Table.descriptors/0 returns (variableform.go, the @tables attribute) carry array_bound but no unbounded_container: `grep -c unbounded_container internal/codegen/elixirtable/*.go` is 0, so a reflection reader cannot tell \"no bound\" from a bound.

THE TASK. Add `unbounded_container: true` with `array_bound: 0` to the descriptor of every map, []T and unbounded list field, `false` with the bound for a bounded [..N]T, in the Table module's descriptors and wherever view.go reflects the same field (§8.1's column, one key per field). Text lists are not carried yet (elixir-carries-bounded-text-lists): leave their rows to those cards and say so in the code. Test TestElixirUnboundedDescriptorIsExplicit in the new unboundeddescriptor_test.go: the Go test's container rows that Elixir carries (map[string(8)]int32, map[int32]int32, []int32, [..4]int32), the two columns per field equal to the Go descriptor's, and the 100000-entry round trip through the binary wire and JSON read back by key and index. Expected answers come from schema-go at d808c3f54 and are never typed from memory: bytes, texts and refusal reasons are read from the engine packages internal/tablewire, internal/tabletext and internal/tablepack inside the Go test (the way internal/codegen/elixirtable/textdifferential_test.go (read) uses them), and where the engine has no surface for the answer the expected value is the one the named Go test pins, cited by file and line in a comment. The Elixir probe is generated into t.TempDir() and run under SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1 (skip by name without the toolchain, fail by name with it), as keyed_test.go does; a Go test that only reads generator output is a unit test, one that runs elixir is a functional test reached from a make target in make/elixir.mk that fails on any SKIP and is listed in test-elixir.

