# Elixir: a deprecated enum variant reads as the field default and is refused on write

RULING (Glenn, 2026-10-18): \"If it's possible to implement, then implement it!\" Enum variants may be deprecated in place (`| deprecated`), one way only. The standard (docs/SPEC-TABLES.md, card spec-enum-variant-deprecated-in-place) and the Go reference (cards schema-go-enum-variant-deprecated-front-end and schema-go-enum-variant-deprecated-codegen) define it; this port matches Go.

PRECONDITION. The front end (parser, ir Variant.Deprecated, check, lock) is shared and lands on sprint/schema-go. If BASE's ir has no Variant.Deprecated, stop at once with Verdict: HOLD, not-done naming the forward-merge of sprint/schema-go into sprint/schema-elixir that is owed; write nothing.

THE TASK. In the Elixir table emitter and runtime: a reader meeting a deprecated variant's id, on every form this port carries (id-table wire, form 3, message form, JSON/text where carried), yields the field's declared default (elements: the element default) and does not count unknown; every writer this port has refuses a value holding a deprecated variant with invalid_value, naming <Table>.<field>; the variant's constant stays, marked deprecated in the language's idiom.

TEST, opening with t.Parallel(), under the port's house pattern (a generated unit run in a t.TempDir()): TestDeprecatedVariantReadsDefaultWriteRefused: a wire written with the variant live reads the default once it is deprecated; each writer refuses; REVERSED WITNESS: the same wire read with the variant live yields the variant. Where Go's reference test gives bytes, this port's reads agree with them.

DONE-WHEN: the test RUNs and PASSes at head (red at BASE: no tests to run) for every form the port carries, with its reversed witness; the port's gate passes or its only reds are named as red at BASE.

STEP 1. cd into the staged checkout JOB.md names and run git log --oneline -1; it is a NEW worktree on the branch this card names. Export GOFLAGS=-mod=readonly NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1 before any go command; GOCACHE is already set to the machine's shared build cache: keep it. Check the PRECONDITION.
STEP 2. Red first: write the test, run it, keep its failing line.
STEP 3. Make it pass in the files PATHS names, citing the section of docs/SPEC-TABLES.md from each function that implements a rule.
STEP 4. Run the gate: go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/elixirtable/ ./compiler/ ./internal/ci/ and read the last line of each. When a test fails, name its file and say whether your work changed it or it is red at BASE, and report that line first.
STEP 5. Commit on your own branch with the trailer. Nothing reaches the forge from inside the wall: the git shim records a push, and the member makes the pull request from outside when the card finishes.
STEP 6. End as JOB.md says; the report starts with Verdict: LAND or Verdict: HOLD on line 1 and Head: <sha> on line 2.

RULES.
Work only in the job directory this card names.
Never force-push or rebase a shared branch.
Never kill a process you did not start.
Never start a server on this machine.
No `rm -rf` outside the job directory.
Report what was not done.
No gh, no push: JOB.md's finish does both, from outside the wall. Never merge. Every `go test` gets `-timeout 600s`; every new test opens with `t.Parallel()`; never `go clean`; never git stash.
Unit tests use no real time, sockets, network or outside services; anything that does is a functional test. A Go test that runs the Elixir toolchain through elixirBinary (internal/codegen/elixirtable/fixedversioning_test.go:589-614) is a functional test: every gate line that runs one sets SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1, and its -v output shows RUN and PASS, never SKIP. Two independent cold reads before merge.
Touch only the files PATHS names. ROADMAP.md, docs/roadmap.sexp and docs/roadmap-evidence/*.sexp are in PATHS by rule: commit the ROADMAP.md that `make roadmap` writes, never a hand edit. make test-elixir rewrites files under generated/: commit the generated/bench/paired/elixir/ files your emitter change moves (regenerate them with make generated/bench/paired/elixir/.stamp), restore any other generated/ file with git checkout and name it in the report. A fix that needs another file goes into the report as a proposed diff, not a commit. \"Not done\" is a welcome report; a green claim you did not run is not.
