# Elixir: a JSON string decoding to U+0000 in text storage refuses the read, text_holds_zero

SOURCE (Glenn, 2026-10-18: \"We do all features across all languages\"). The standard is docs/SPEC-TABLES.md at d808c3f54 (schema-go), which arrives on this base with elixir-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f; schema-go's behaviour there is the oracle. Never write spec text: a section that is silent or stale is reported, not edited. Family other rules, text zero byte: docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §16.3 (and §2.13, §4.2): a JSON string the read places in text storage that decodes to U+0000 refuses with malformed and reason `text_holds_zero: <Table>.<field>` for the innermost field; oracle internal/codegen/gotable/textzero_test.go TestGoTextZeroByteTakesOneRuleInEveryForm. Needs the report's reason from elixir-plain-load-is-the-strict-read.

RED AT BASE (BASE is the base after elixir-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f landed). from_json lands `\\u0000` in a string(8) field silently: the zero check utf8_valid?/1 (variableform.go:1921) runs on the binary read paths (1047, 1142, 1610) but no JSON string path calls it, and `grep -c text_holds_zero internal/codegen/elixirtable/*.go` is 0.

THE TASK. Refuse a decoded U+0000 placed in text storage on every JSON read path (string(N), *string, a text-list element once carried, a map key, a wstring(N), a nested table's string), with report.malformed and reason `text_holds_zero: <Table>.<field>`; a zero only SKIPPED (an unknown key's value, an object key naming no field) is not the event, and `\\u0001` reads clean. Test TestElixirTextZeroByteTakesOneRuleInEveryForm in the new textzero_test.go: the Go test's rows Elixir carries (front, middle and end of each storage kind), the verdict and reason equal to Go's, the \\u0001 control round-tripping. Expected answers come from schema-go at d808c3f54 and are never typed from memory: bytes, texts and refusal reasons are read from the engine packages internal/tablewire, internal/tabletext and internal/tablepack inside the Go test (the way internal/codegen/elixirtable/textdifferential_test.go (read) uses them), and where the engine has no surface for the answer the expected value is the one the named Go test pins, cited by file and line in a comment. The Elixir probe is generated into t.TempDir() and run under SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1 (skip by name without the toolchain, fail by name with it), as keyed_test.go does; a Go test that only reads generator output is a unit test, one that runs elixir is a functional test reached from a make target in make/elixir.mk that fails on any SKIP and is listed in test-elixir.

