# Elixir: the plain variable-form read is the strict read; the tolerant read is a named twin

SOURCE (Glenn, 2026-10-18: \"We do all features across all languages\"). The standard is docs/SPEC-TABLES.md at d808c3f54 (schema-go), which arrives on this base with elixir-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f; schema-go's behaviour there is the oracle. Never write spec text: a section that is silent or stale is reported, not edited. Family strict-read: docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §6.5 (THE PLAIN NAME IS THE STRICT READ; THE TOLERANT READ HAS A NAME OF ITS OWN) and §11; oracle internal/codegen/gotable/strictdefault_test.go TestGoDefaultReadIsStrict.

RED AT BASE (BASE is the base after elixir-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f landed). internal/codegen/elixirtable/variableform.go emits `load/2` and `from_json/2` on the Table module as TOLERANT reads: its moduledoc says \"`load/2` reads one tolerantly\" (variableform.go:72) and a clamped, kind-mismatched or partly malformed body answers `{:ok, value, report}` with the counters set (variableform.go:822-846, from_json at 1972-2004); the report map has no reason key (empty_report at 1750-1760).

THE TASK. Make the plain names strict and add the twins, in the Elixir spelling §6.5 names (a tagged tuple): `load/2` and `from_json/2` answer `{:error, reason, report}` whenever the read's report holds what ReadStrict refuses (malformed, clamped, kind_mismatch, truncated framing; unknown, widened and retained alone do not refuse), with `reason` the atom of §6.5's TableRefuseReason spelling (`:clamped`, `:kind_mismatch`, `:malformed`, ...), and never hand back the recovered value; `load_tolerant/2` and `from_json_tolerant/2` keep today's behaviour exactly. Add a `reason` key to the report (nil on a clean read). Move every in-repo caller that relies on tolerance (the conformance driver test/conformance/elixir/driver_impl.ex, the card and fuzz probes make/elixir.mk runs) to the tolerant twin, so their outputs are byte-identical. missing_required has no Elixir carrier yet (elixir-carries-required) and blob_shared and non_finite are their own cards: leave room in the reason set, do not add them here. Test TestElixirDefaultReadIsStrict in the new strictdefault_test.go: over a variable root (tables/examples Guarded or a probe unit), a clean, a clamped, a kind-mismatched and a truncated wire, each through load/2 and load_tolerant/2 and the same values through from_json/2 and from_json_tolerant/2; the strict calls refuse with the reason the Go engine's strict read names for the same bytes and the tolerant calls return the value and counters the engine's tolerant read reports. Expected answers come from schema-go at d808c3f54 and are never typed from memory: bytes, texts and refusal reasons are read from the engine packages internal/tablewire, internal/tabletext and internal/tablepack inside the Go test (the way internal/codegen/elixirtable/textdifferential_test.go (read) uses them), and where the engine has no surface for the answer the expected value is the one the named Go test pins, cited by file and line in a comment. The Elixir probe is generated into t.TempDir() and run under SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1 (skip by name without the toolchain, fail by name with it), as keyed_test.go does; a Go test that only reads generator output is a unit test, one that runs elixir is a functional test reached from a make target in make/elixir.mk that fails on any SKIP and is listed in test-elixir.

DONE-WHEN: SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1 go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/elixirtable -run '^TestElixirDefaultReadIsStrict$' -v shows RUN and --- PASS, and the same command at BASE prints `testing: warning: no tests to run` (the test is new) while the gap named under RED AT BASE holds there; the conformance harness's elixir column (./build/conformance-harness run --only elixir) and make cards-elixir-test, cards-elixir-wire-fuzz and cards-elixir-retain-fuzz stay green with unchanged outputs; the committed generated Elixir is current (make test-elixir's generated-current and format checks green); make roadmap-check is clean.

