# One blob record named from two slots: Java's writers refuse it, the tolerant read counts it, the strict read refuses blob_shared

SOURCE (Glenn, 2026-10-18): \"We do all features across all languages.\" Full parity of the Java port with schema-go at d808c3f54 (the oracle, which reaches this base through java-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f). The standard is docs/SPEC-TABLES.md and docs/USAGE.md at that commit.

THE RULE. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §2.5 and §16.7, and the blob_shared row of §6.5's TableRefuseReason table: one blob record named from two slots is refused by every writer, kept once and counted by the tolerant read, and refused `blob_shared` by the strict read; the amplification it would allow is the reason.

THE GAP AT BASE. `grep -c 'blob_shared\\|blobShared' internal/codegen/javatable/*.go` is 0 at BASE: a wire whose two *string slots reference one blob record loads in Java with both slots filled and nothing counted or refused.

THE TASK. Track blob node references in the Java load (no new allocation per node: a bit per node in the directory the load already holds), count the second reference in the tolerant read, refuse blob_shared in the strict read, and make every writer that could emit a shared blob (save, retain save) refuse as Go's writers do. Cover the message form if Go's MessageForm case applies to a form the Java port carries.

TESTS. A Go test that only reads the generator's output is a unit test: it uses no real time, sockets, network or outside services. A test that compiles or runs Java (javac, java) is a functional test: it finds the JDK through javaTools (internal/codegen/javatable/fixedform_test.go), skips by name when there is none and SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS is unset, fails by name when SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1. Expected answers come from schema-go: generate the same probe schema for Go in the same test (the gotable runGenerated pattern, or the engine packages internal/tablewire and internal/tabletext) and compare bytes, reports and reason names; never type an expected byte by hand. Every new test opens with t.Parallel().

