# Java's table descriptors carry the type name, wire id, exact integer range bounds and an explicit unbounded container

SOURCE (Glenn, 2026-10-18): \"We do all features across all languages.\" Full parity of the Java port with schema-go at d808c3f54 (the oracle, which reaches this base through java-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f). The standard is docs/SPEC-TABLES.md and docs/USAGE.md at that commit.

THE RULE. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §8.1: field name, type name, wire id and kind, element size, array bound, declared bounds; integer range bounds exact (RangeIntMin/Max, RangeUintMax; no float rounding past 2^53; an int128 carries no int64 pair: TestGoExactIntegerRangeBounds, df1c21086); an unbounded container is explicit in the descriptor, not a zero bound (§2.9, TestGoUnboundedDescriptorIsExplicit).

THE GAP AT BASE. At BASE view.go writes four columns per field (name, kind, shape, bound) and a bound of 0 for an unbounded list; `grep -c -i 'range\\|unbounded\\|wireId' internal/codegen/javatable/view.go` finds only the 'unbounded list' comment.

THE TASK. Add the columns to TableFieldInfo and the generated descriptors for every table of the unit (not only the card closure), spelling the range bounds as exact longs (an unsigned max as its two's-complement long with an unsigned accessor), and an `unbounded` boolean.

TESTS. A Go test that only reads the generator's output is a unit test: it uses no real time, sockets, network or outside services. A test that compiles or runs Java (javac, java) is a functional test: it finds the JDK through javaTools (internal/codegen/javatable/fixedform_test.go), skips by name when there is none and SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS is unset, fails by name when SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1. Expected answers come from schema-go: generate the same probe schema for Go in the same test (the gotable runGenerated pattern, or the engine packages internal/tablewire and internal/tabletext) and compare bytes, reports and reason names; never type an expected byte by hand. Every new test opens with t.Parallel().

