# A \\u0000 in Java's JSON text takes the one rule every form takes: the read is refused, malformed

SOURCE (Glenn, 2026-10-18): \"We do all features across all languages.\" Full parity of the Java port with schema-go at d808c3f54 (the oracle, which reaches this base through java-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f). The standard is docs/SPEC-TABLES.md and docs/USAGE.md at that commit.

THE RULE. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §16.3 with §3/§4: a zero byte is not text in any form. A \\u0000 decoded into a string(N), *string, text-list element, wstring or text map key makes the JSON read malformed (one Malformed, no kind mismatch), exactly as the wire's textValid refuses it; a \\u0001 control reads clean; a zero inside a value the read only SKIPS is not the event.

THE GAP AT BASE. At BASE Java's scanString (variableform.go near line 1943) appends the decoded U+0000 and the field readers store it, so fromJson accepts `{\"name\":\"a\\u0000b\"}` silently while the wire writer's textValid would refuse the same string.

THE TASK. Make every JSON text destination (string(N), *string, wstring, list elements, map keys; text-list shapes once the port carries them) refuse a decoded U+0000 as malformed, and keep skip paths (unknown keys, unknown arm payloads) unaffected. Match Go's verdict and report for every planted position.

TESTS. A Go test that only reads the generator's output is a unit test: it uses no real time, sockets, network or outside services. A test that compiles or runs Java (javac, java) is a functional test: it finds the JDK through javaTools (internal/codegen/javatable/fixedform_test.go), skips by name when there is none and SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS is unset, fails by name when SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1. Expected answers come from schema-go: generate the same probe schema for Go in the same test (the gotable runGenerated pattern, or the engine packages internal/tablewire and internal/tabletext) and compare bytes, reports and reason names; never type an expected byte by hand. Every new test opens with t.Parallel().

LEDGERS AND GENERATED FILES. ROADMAP.md, docs/roadmap.sexp, docs/roadmap-evidence/*.sexp, every *_test.go, every testdata/ file and AGENTS.md are inside every card's PATHS by rule. `make roadmap` and `make roadmap-check` need sbcl (tools/roadmap/roadmap.mk): when either refuses with \"sbcl is not installed\", paste that line, report the step not-done and never hand-edit ROADMAP.md. `make test-java` and `make conformance` rewrite generated/ in the worktree: restore what the change did not move (git checkout -- generated/<path>) and name in the pull request body every generated/ path the change moved, for the coordinator's regeneration at the stream merge. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md and docs/USAGE.md are the standard and are never edited by this card: a rule the standard does not state goes into the report as a finding, not into the text.

DONE-WHEN: (1) `SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1 go test -count=1 -timeout 600s -v ./internal/codegen/javatable/ -run '^TestJavaTextZeroByteTakesOneRuleInEveryForm$'` prints RUN and PASS and no SKIP at the head, and the same command at the unchanged BASE prints `no tests to run` (the test is new) while the BASE-side probe the test holds fails (paste both lines); (2) the test plants \\u0000 at the front, middle and end of each text shape the Java port carries, and \\u0001 in the same places, and compares Java's verdict and report with Go's generated FromJson for the same schema and text; (3) `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/javatable/ ./compiler/` is green or its only reds are named as present at BASE (with the BASE run); `make test-java JAVA=\"$JAVA\" JAVAC=\"$JAVAC\"` has no red target the BASE run does not also have (each named); make roadmap-check is clean.

