# A plain float's -0, NaN and infinities take one rule in every Java form

SOURCE (Glenn, 2026-10-18): \"We do all features across all languages.\" Full parity of the Java port with schema-go at d808c3f54 (the oracle, which reaches this base through java-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f). The standard is docs/SPEC-TABLES.md and docs/USAGE.md at that commit.

THE RULE. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §3.3, \"A PLAIN FLOAT'S SPECIAL VALUES TAKE ONE RULE\": the default elision compares BIT PATTERNS, so -0 rides the variable and message forms and reads back -0; a NaN or infinity rides every binary form bit for bit; the tolerant variable read names it non_finite and counts nothing; the strict read refuses it (non_finite: <Table>.<field>), because the JSON writer has no spelling for one (§16.3).

THE GAP AT BASE. At BASE the variable writer elides with `v.x != def` (variableform.go near lines 3989 and 3992), so -0.0 equals the 0 default and is dropped, and it writes Float.floatToIntBits / Double.doubleToLongBits, which canonicalise every NaN payload; `grep -c 'non_finite\\|nonFinite' internal/codegen/javatable/*.go` is 0.

THE TASK. Elide on raw bits (floatToRawIntBits / doubleToRawLongBits compared with the default's bits) and write raw bits in every variable and retain writer path (scalar, list element, map value, union arm, optional); the tolerant read names non_finite in report.reason and counts nothing; the strict plain load and fromJson refuse non_finite naming the field as Go does. Assert on raw bits throughout.

TESTS. A Go test that only reads the generator's output is a unit test: it uses no real time, sockets, network or outside services. A test that compiles or runs Java (javac, java) is a functional test: it finds the JDK through javaTools (internal/codegen/javatable/fixedform_test.go), skips by name when there is none and SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS is unset, fails by name when SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1. Expected answers come from schema-go: generate the same probe schema for Go in the same test (the gotable runGenerated pattern, or the engine packages internal/tablewire and internal/tabletext) and compare bytes, reports and reason names; never type an expected byte by hand. Every new test opens with t.Parallel().

LEDGERS AND GENERATED FILES. ROADMAP.md, docs/roadmap.sexp, docs/roadmap-evidence/*.sexp, every *_test.go, every testdata/ file and AGENTS.md are inside every card's PATHS by rule. `make roadmap` and `make roadmap-check` need sbcl (tools/roadmap/roadmap.mk): when either refuses with \"sbcl is not installed\", paste that line, report the step not-done and never hand-edit ROADMAP.md. `make test-java` and `make conformance` rewrite generated/ in the worktree: restore what the change did not move (git checkout -- generated/<path>) and name in the pull request body every generated/ path the change moved, for the coordinator's regeneration at the stream merge. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md and docs/USAGE.md are the standard and are never edited by this card: a rule the standard does not state goes into the report as a finding, not into the text.

DONE-WHEN: (1) `SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1 go test -count=1 -timeout 600s -v ./internal/codegen/javatable/ -run '^TestJavaPlainFloatSpecialValuesAgreeAcrossForms$'` prints RUN and PASS and no SKIP at the head, and the same command at the unchanged BASE prints `no tests to run` (the test is new) while the BASE-side probe the test holds fails (paste both lines); (2) the test runs the same schema and the same planted -0, NaN (two payloads) and +/-inf as the Go test through Java's variable wire, message form and fixed form, comparing the saved bytes with Go's byte for byte and the strict/tolerant verdicts and reason names with Go's; (3) `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/javatable/ ./compiler/` is green or its only reds are named as present at BASE (with the BASE run); `make test-java JAVA=\"$JAVA\" JAVAC=\"$JAVAC\"` has no red target the BASE run does not also have (each named); make roadmap-check is clean.

