# Java's plain load and fromJson are the strict read; loadTolerant and fromJsonTolerant are the named twins

SOURCE (Glenn, 2026-10-18): \"We do all features across all languages.\" Full parity of the Java port with schema-go at d808c3f54 (the oracle, which reaches this base through java-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f). The standard is docs/SPEC-TABLES.md and docs/USAGE.md at that commit.

THE RULE. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §6.5 \"The two load paths\" and §11: THE PLAIN NAME IS THE STRICT READ. A plain read refuses whatever the strict read refuses (clamped, kind_mismatch, truncated/wire_damaged, malformed, missing_required where the construct exists) and hands back no recovered value; the reason reaches the caller by name; the tolerant read keeps the recovered value and its counters under the explicit `...Tolerant` name. unknown, widened and retained alone do not refuse.

THE GAP AT BASE. `grep -c -i 'tolerant' internal/codegen/javatable/variableform.go` finds only comments at BASE: there is no loadTolerant or fromJsonTolerant, and the plain `load(byte[], Report)` returns the recovered Value with report.clamped / kindMismatch / malformed set and no refusal.

THE TASK. Rename the emitted tolerant bodies to loadTolerant and fromJsonTolerant (same signatures), and emit plain load and fromJson as the strict wrappers: run the tolerant read into the caller's Report (a fresh one when null), and when it holds clamped, kind_mismatch, malformed or a refusal answer null with report.refuse set to the TableRefuseReason Go's report.Err() names for the same input (clamped, kind_mismatch, malformed, truncated, wire_damaged). The retain load answers its root as loadTolerant does (§6.5, bcff9c99e). Move every in-repo Java caller that relied on tolerance (tables/cards/java (read), bench/, test/conformance/java/src/Driver.java (read), test/java-tables (read)) to the tolerant name where it reads damaged input on purpose; those files are named in the pull request body as proposed diffs if outside PATHS.

TESTS. A Go test that only reads the generator's output is a unit test: it uses no real time, sockets, network or outside services. A test that compiles or runs Java (javac, java) is a functional test: it finds the JDK through javaTools (internal/codegen/javatable/fixedform_test.go), skips by name when there is none and SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS is unset, fails by name when SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1. Expected answers come from schema-go: generate the same probe schema for Go in the same test (the gotable runGenerated pattern, or the engine packages internal/tablewire and internal/tabletext) and compare bytes, reports and reason names; never type an expected byte by hand. Every new test opens with t.Parallel().

