# Java's timestamp JSON text is strict RFC 3339 section 5.6 date-time

SOURCE (Glenn, 2026-10-18): \"We do all features across all languages.\" Full parity of the Java port with schema-go at d808c3f54 (the oracle, which reaches this base through java-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f). The standard is docs/SPEC-TABLES.md and docs/USAGE.md at that commit.

THE RULE. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §2.11 and §16.2: written with a Z offset, read from exactly the RFC 3339 §5.6 date-time production (year 0000-9999, day valid for the month, T or t, second 00-59, a fraction truncated to nanoseconds, Z/z or an offset with hours 00-23); anything else, or an instant outside the nanosecond domain, is refused by name `timestamp_not_rfc3339: <field>`; a NUMBER token is a kind mismatch, skipped and counted.

THE GAP AT BASE. At BASE no Java JSON path reads or writes a timestamp (the previous card refuses it by name until this one).

THE TASK. Write and read the strict spelling by hand (java.time's parsers accept spellings §5.6 does not: do not use them as the validator), reusing internal/rfc3339 (read)'s case table as the test oracle.

TESTS. A Go test that only reads the generator's output is a unit test: it uses no real time, sockets, network or outside services. A test that compiles or runs Java (javac, java) is a functional test: it finds the JDK through javaTools (internal/codegen/javatable/fixedform_test.go), skips by name when there is none and SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS is unset, fails by name when SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1. Expected answers come from schema-go: generate the same probe schema for Go in the same test (the gotable runGenerated pattern, or the engine packages internal/tablewire and internal/tabletext) and compare bytes, reports and reason names; never type an expected byte by hand. Every new test opens with t.Parallel().

LEDGERS AND GENERATED FILES. ROADMAP.md, docs/roadmap.sexp, docs/roadmap-evidence/*.sexp, every *_test.go, every testdata/ file and AGENTS.md are inside every card's PATHS by rule. `make roadmap` and `make roadmap-check` need sbcl (tools/roadmap/roadmap.mk): when either refuses with \"sbcl is not installed\", paste that line, report the step not-done and never hand-edit ROADMAP.md. `make test-java` and `make conformance` rewrite generated/ in the worktree: restore what the change did not move (git checkout -- generated/<path>) and name in the pull request body every generated/ path the change moved, for the coordinator's regeneration at the stream merge. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md and docs/USAGE.md are the standard and are never edited by this card: a rule the standard does not state goes into the report as a finding, not into the text.

DONE-WHEN: (1) `SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1 go test -count=1 -timeout 600s -v ./internal/codegen/javatable/ -run '^TestJavaTimestampStrictRFC3339$'` prints RUN and PASS and no SKIP at the head, and the same command at the unchanged BASE prints `no tests to run` (the test is new) while the BASE-side probe the test holds fails (paste both lines); (2) every case of internal/rfc3339's TestStrictRFC3339 table gives Java's fromJson the same verdict and nanosecond value as Go's FromJson, and toJson equals Go's text; (3) `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/javatable/ ./compiler/` is green or its only reds are named as present at BASE (with the BASE run); `make test-java JAVA=\"$JAVA\" JAVAC=\"$JAVAC\"` has no red target the BASE run does not also have (each named); make roadmap-check is clean.

STEP 1. cd into the staged checkout JOB.md names and run git log --oneline -1; it is a NEW worktree on the branch this card names. Export GOFLAGS=-mod=readonly NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1 before any go command; GOCACHE is already set to the machine's shared build cache (JOB.md names it): keep it. Run `. ~/sdk/env.sh` when it exists and export JAVA and JAVAC as the bench JDK's java and javac; pass JAVA=\"$JAVA\" JAVAC=\"$JAVAC\" to every make, because make/java.mk's defaults (lines 15-16) are a macOS dist path. Build and test only on the Linux bench JOB.md names, never the working Mac.
