# JavaScript: one blob named from two slots is written by no writer, counted by the tolerant read and refused by the strict read

THE RULE. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §2.5 (\"A blob is OWNED BY ONE SLOT\"), §6.5 (blob_shared), §16.7: every writer refuses to emit a wire where two slots name one blob record (Measure -1 and Save null with reason blob_shared; the text writer refuses the graph); the TOLERANT read keeps the value with both slots naming the one node it decoded and counts each extra naming in a `blobShared` counter; the STRICT read refuses `blob_shared`. Oracle: internal/codegen/gotable/blobnamedtwice_test.go (TestBlobNamedTwiceSeed24, TestBlobNamedTwiceWriters, TestBlobNamedTwiceAmplification).

THE GAP AT BASE. internal/codegen/jstable/variableruntime.go's header comment (lines 31-32) and TableVariableResolve (line 592, `n.shared = new String(n.blob)`) KEEP a shared blob as one String object and say \"a load-save of a shared blob writes it once again\": the JS writer emits the shared wire, and the read counts nothing.

THE TASK. Make Measure and Save refuse a value whose graph names one blob from two slots (reason blob_shared, by the walk that numbers the nodes, TableVariableNumber); count each extra naming on the tolerant read (a new blobShared counter on TableVariableReport) and refuse it on the strict read (this card's need chain brings the strict read and the reason channel). The text writer already refuses the graph: keep it. Rewrite any test or corpus expectation in this package that pinned the old write-it-again behaviour, and say which.

THE TESTS. TestJsBlobNamedTwice (new) uses the oracle's seed-24 unit and wire and its writer cases: the tolerant read's value and counter, the strict read's refusal, and Measure/Save refusing, each equal to Go's answers from the Go leg. Red at BASE.

THE ORACLE. schema-go at d808c3f54 (merged into this base by js-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f) is the expected answer for every case; never write spec text, and never take an expected answer from this leg's own output. Get the Go answer the way internal/codegen/jstable/cardsretain_test.go goRetainedSave does: generate the SAME schema with internal/codegen/golang and internal/codegen/gotable into t.TempDir(), run a small Go driver there with `go test -count=1`, and read its printed answers (wire bytes as hex, the report's counters and reason); or, where the rule lives in an engine package (internal/tablewire, internal/tabletext), call the engine directly as textdifferential_test.go does. The JavaScript side runs the generated module in node through jsNode(t), exactly as the existing tests in this package do. Copy the oracle test's schema and cases (named below) so the two legs answer the same inputs.

DONE-WHEN: (1) TestJsBlobNamedTwice passes and fails at BASE; (2) the comment and code at variableruntime.go:31-32 and :592 that keep the shared String are gone (grep `writes it once again` matches at BASE, not at the head); (3) the js conformance column unchanged or better, and if the tables/blobs corpus's shared-blob instance now reads as refused on the strict surface, say so (§2.5 records the corpus as differing from the standard: do not edit the corpus); (4) the STEP 4 gate green or reds named as present at BASE.

