# JavaScript's block form carries the wide kinds: 128-bit integers and fixed point

THE RULE. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §19 (the block form) and §15's wide kinds: Go's block form lays out and reads `int128`/`uint128` and `fixed(I, F)` fields; ir.WideTableKinds (ir/tablekind.go) is the scope rule for a port that does not carry them yet. Oracle: internal/codegen/gotable/block.go and blockbuild.go at d808c3f54 (the Go block image of the same declaration).

THE GAP AT BASE. internal/codegen/jstable/jstable.go GenerateLineage skips generateBlockFiles for any unit with a wide field (`if len(wide) == 0 { ... generateBlockFiles ... }`), refusing the block module by name.

THE TASK. Carry the wide kinds in block.go's row accessors and builder (BigInt values, sixteen bytes low half first for 128-bit; the raw scaled integer at its storage width for fixed point, as Go's block does), and let a wide unit get its <Base>Block.js (the gate in jstable.go GenerateLineage, split so the cook stays withheld for a wide unit until js-cook-carries-the-wide-kinds lands).

THE TESTS. TestJsBlockCarriesWideKinds (new): a block of a fixed table with int128, uint128, fixed(16, 16) and fixed(32, 32) fields built by the Go leg's builder is read by the JS accessors to the same values, and the JS builder's image equals Go's byte for byte. Red at BASE (no block module for the unit).

