# JavaScript: the plain FixedLoad is strict; FixedLoadTolerant keeps today's answers

THE RULE. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §6.5 (THE PLAIN NAME IS THE STRICT READ): the plain `<Name>FixedLoad` refuses (answers -1) whatever the strict read refuses, resets every value it had written, and the reason reaches the caller through the report; `<Name>FixedLoadTolerant` keeps the signature and the answers the plain name had. Per §3.3's float table a fixed read of a NaN or infinity is NOT a refusal. Oracle: TestGoDefaultReadIsStrict's \"fixed\" subtest (internal/codegen/gotable/strictdefault_test.go).

THE GAP AT BASE. internal/codegen/jstable/fixedmodule.go emits `<Name>FixedLoad(values, capacity, bytes, byteLength, plan, report)`, which counts `report.clamped` (fixedjs.go emitDecodeBounds) and returns the record count with the clamped values: a ranged value past its bound reads clamped and is not refused. No FixedLoadTolerant exists.

THE TASK. Rename the emitted read to `<Name>FixedLoadTolerant` (same signature, same answers), and emit `<Name>FixedLoad` as the strict read: run the tolerant read, and when its report holds an error (malformed, clamped, kind_mismatch, or any named refusal) reset every value it had written to its declared defaults and answer -1, the reason on the report as Go spells it. Update the in-repo callers (test/js-tables/fixedform.mjs, test/js-tables/main.mjs) to the twin each means; if the conformance driver test/conformance/js/main.mjs (read) calls FixedLoad and a surface moves, put its diff in the report as PATHS-PROPOSED (js-plain-load-messages-is-strict owns that file).

THE TESTS. TestJsFixedLoadIsStrict (new) uses the oracle's fixed schema (`fixed table Fix { n int32 = 1 | min = 0, max = 10 ...}` and its clean, clamped and truncated files, saved by the Go leg) and asserts the plain FixedLoad's answer, the reset values and the reason equal Go's, and the Tolerant twin's count, values and counters equal Go's FixedLoadTolerant. Red at BASE.

THE ORACLE. schema-go at d808c3f54 (merged into this base by js-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f) is the expected answer for every case; never write spec text, and never take an expected answer from this leg's own output. Get the Go answer the way internal/codegen/jstable/cardsretain_test.go goRetainedSave does: generate the SAME schema with internal/codegen/golang and internal/codegen/gotable into t.TempDir(), run a small Go driver there with `go test -count=1`, and read its printed answers (wire bytes as hex, the report's counters and reason); or, where the rule lives in an engine package (internal/tablewire, internal/tabletext), call the engine directly as textdifferential_test.go does. The JavaScript side runs the generated module in node through jsNode(t), exactly as the existing tests in this package do. Copy the oracle test's schema and cases (named below) so the two legs answer the same inputs.

