# JavaScript: the plain LoadMessages is strict; LoadMessagesTolerant keeps today's answers

THE RULE. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §6.5: the message read `<Name>LoadMessages` is a plain name and refuses whatever the strict read refuses, answering ok == false; its count is 0 when the strict rule alone refused (every value it had written reset) and what the tolerant read answered when the tolerant read itself stopped on a body (the records ahead of it keep what they decoded). `<Name>LoadMessagesTolerant` keeps today's signature and answers. Oracle: TestGoDefaultReadIsStrict (the fixed root's LoadMessages leg) and internal/codegen/gotable/message*.go at d808c3f54.

THE GAP AT BASE. internal/codegen/jstable/messagebatch.go:852 emits `<Name>LoadMessages(values, vocabulary, data, report)` as the tolerant read: a clamped body lands clamped and counts. There is no Tolerant twin.

THE TASK. Rename the emitted read to `<Name>LoadMessagesTolerant` and emit the strict `<Name>LoadMessages` over it with the answers above, the reason on the report as Go spells it. Update the in-repo callers (test/conformance/js/main.mjs surfaceMessage, the jstable tests) to the twin each means, so the `message` surface does not move.

THE TESTS. TestJsLoadMessagesIsStrict (new) builds the oracle's batches with the Go leg (a clean batch, one with a clamped body, one damaged mid-batch) and asserts the plain and tolerant answers (ok, count, values, counters, reason) equal Go's. Red at BASE.

