# JavaScript: the plain variable read is the strict read; the tolerant read has its own name

THE RULE. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §6.5 (\"THE PLAIN NAME IS THE STRICT READ; THE TOLERANT READ HAS A NAME OF ITS OWN\", and \"The STRICT read\") and §11: every generated read entry point a caller reaches by the plain name refuses whatever the strict read refuses (malformed, clamped, kind_mismatch, an unknown form, wire damage, build-version mismatch, missing_required) and hands back no recovered value; the tolerant read keeps the recovered value and its report under an explicit `...Tolerant` name; `<Name>ReadStrict(wire)` answers the root or refuses with one typed reason; `unknown`, `widened` and `retained` alone never refuse. Oracle: internal/codegen/gotable/strictdefault_test.go TestGoDefaultReadIsStrict and commits 61105d0b1, 1f53ff49d, f995112ac, bcff9c99e, 2c0700baa.

THE GAP AT BASE. internal/codegen/jstable/variableform.go emitRootCodec emits `<Name>Load(bytes, report)` and `<Name>FromJson(text, report)` as the TOLERANT reads (TableVariableLoad, TableVariableFromJson): a clamped or kind-mismatched wire returns the recovered value with the counter set. `grep -rn Tolerant internal/codegen/jstable` finds nothing, there is no ReadStrict, and TableVariableReport carries counters but no reason.

THE TASK. For every variable-form root (the module <Package>Variable.js, which also carries the fixed-class tables' variable read): (1) rename today's behaviour to `<Name>LoadTolerant(bytes, report)` and `<Name>FromJsonTolerant(text, report)`, signatures and answers unchanged; (2) make `<Name>Load` and `<Name>FromJson` strict: decide on THIS read alone, overwrite a supplied report with this read's counters, answer null (no value) when the read's report holds an error, and set the reason so the caller can read it; (3) add `report.err()` on TableVariableReport answering the reason string by Go's spelling (`malformed`, `clamped`, `kind_mismatch`, `unknown_form`, `wire_damaged`, `truncated`, `build_version_mismatch` where the Go reason exists) or null on a clean read and on a read whose only counters are unknown, widened or retained; (4) emit `<Name>ReadStrict(bytes)` answering the value or throwing a JavaScript Error whose `.reason` is that same string (the language's standard error idiom, §6.5 WHERE IT IS CARRIED). Update every in-repo caller that relied on the tolerant plain name (test/conformance/js/main.mjs and the jstable tests) to call the twin it means, so no conformance surface moves.

THE TESTS. TestJsDefaultReadIsStrict (new internal/codegen/jstable/strictdefault_test.go) drives the oracle's variable-root cases (a clean, a clamped, a kind-mismatched and a truncated wire, built as the Go test builds them) through the plain Load, FromJson, LoadTolerant, FromJsonTolerant and ReadStrict, and asserts per case that the plain name refuses exactly when Go's plain name refuses, with the same reason string Go's report.Err() gives, and that the tolerant twin returns the same recovered value and counters Go's LoadTolerant gives. It fails at BASE (no Tolerant name, no err()).

THE ORACLE. schema-go at d808c3f54 (merged into this base by js-base-takes-schema-go-d808c3f) is the expected answer for every case; never write spec text, and never take an expected answer from this leg's own output. Get the Go answer the way internal/codegen/jstable/cardsretain_test.go goRetainedSave does: generate the SAME schema with internal/codegen/golang and internal/codegen/gotable into t.TempDir(), run a small Go driver there with `go test -count=1`, and read its printed answers (wire bytes as hex, the report's counters and reason); or, where the rule lives in an engine package (internal/tablewire, internal/tabletext), call the engine directly as textdifferential_test.go does. The JavaScript side runs the generated module in node through jsNode(t), exactly as the existing tests in this package do. Copy the oracle test's schema and cases (named below) so the two legs answer the same inputs.

DONE-WHEN: (1) TestJsDefaultReadIsStrict passes at the head and fails at BASE (keep the failing line); (2) `grep -c Tolerant internal/codegen/jstable/variableform.go` is 0 at BASE and positive at the head; (3) the js column of `make NODE=node conformance` is unchanged or better, per-surface counts before and after; (4) the gate in STEP 4 is green or its reds are named as present at BASE; (5) generated/ files that moved are named.

