THE RULE. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §2.13: `[N]string(M)`, `[E]string(M)`, `[..N]string(M)` and the optional element `[..N]?string(M)` on the variable wire and in JSON, with the descriptor's TextList column (§8.1). Oracle: TestGoTableStringArraysAndJSONArrayRoot's text-list rows (gotable/stringarrays_test.go); refusal test compiler/stringarrays_refusal_test.go (TestStringArraysRefusedOffGo), whose array-root row stays refused until rust-carries-the-json-array-root.

THE TASK. Carry the four spellings in the variable codec and the JSON form; refuseTextForms keeps refusing rust for the array root and for unbounded text (§2.13.1, the next card). The new test runs the Go test's text-list schemas and values through wire and JSON and asserts the Go bytes and texts. Register rust as a carrier of this construct: add the key `text-list` to rust's registerCarries call in compiler/carries_rust.go, the one per-target file schema-go-target-names-what-it-carries defines (PATHS reaches it as compiler/*_rust.go; nothing else of that card's is edited), so the central refusal in compiler/generate.go (read) (refuseTextForms) no longer refuses rust and still refuses every other non-Go target: the compiler test that holds the refusal is updated to treat rust as a carrier, the way it treats go.

TESTS. A Go test that only reads the generator's output is a unit test: it uses no real time, sockets, network or outside services. A test that compiles or runs Rust (rustc, cargo) is a functional test: it skips by name when rustc is absent and SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS is unset, fails by name when SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS=1, and runs on the bench with that variable set, so it never passes on a SKIP. Use the leg's existing harness: the rusttable functional-test pattern (a generated crate built with cargo beside a Go oracle, as retainroots_test.go and messageform_batch_test.go do) and the conformance driver test/conformance/rust/src/main.rs where a surface answers; never a new harness.

