# C ludicrous test pins the int128 sign extension of a negative int64

TEST-ONLY. No emitter, runtime or generated file is edited.

THE GAP. test/c-ludicrous/main.c:153 and :167 widen serialize_int128_from_int64(-12345678901234) into DegenerateProbe.locked_wide and compare the read value with the same expression. locked_wide is a degenerate (min == max) field that costs ZERO bits on the wire (SPEC §4.6), so the value never reaches the wire, and the check compares the helper's result with itself: a from64 helper that drops the sign extension still passes.

THE TASK. Leave the DegenerateProbe block's degenerate checks as they are, and add a check that pins the sign-extended bytes: for -1, INT64_MIN and -4294967301, assert the high and low 64-bit halves of serialize_int128_from_int64(v) against literal constants (high = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF for each; low = the two's-complement of v), and round-trip each through a field that really carries 128 bits on the wire (the int128 field the file already writes at :77, in.wide.energy, or another non-degenerate int128 field of the Ludicrous schema), asserting the read halves against the same literals, never against a second call of the helper. Add a planted check: when main.c is built with -DLUDICROUS_PLANT_FROM64_ZERO_EXTEND, a macro defined only under that flag replaces serialize_int128_from_int64 with a zero-extending version (high half 0), and the new checks must fail.

SIBLING, NOT IN THIS CARD. test/cs-ludicrous/src/Program.cs (read) at :235 and :247 have the same vacuous shape (LockedWide = -12345678901234L on DegenerateProbe, compared with the same cast); it is named here for a C# card and is not in this card's PATHS.

DONE-WHEN: (1) make build/schema_test_c_ludicrous, then build/schema_test_c_ludicrous run from inside test/c-ludicrous as make/c.mk (read) line 1142 runs it, passes at head; (2) the same build with CFLAGS adding -DLUDICROUS_PLANT_FROM64_ZERO_EXTEND (a scratch build line in the report, never committed to make/c.mk) fails at head on the new checks, naming -1, INT64_MIN and -4294967301, and passes at BASE (the vacuous check); keep both lines; (3) the wire goldens of the ludicrous suite are unchanged, or any new golden is written by the suite's own update path and named; (4) go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/ci/ green or its reds named as present at BASE.

STEP 1. cd into the staged checkout JOB.md names and run git log --oneline -1; it is a NEW worktree on the branch this card names. Export GOFLAGS=-mod=readonly NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1 before any go command; GOCACHE is already set to the machine's shared build cache (JOB.md names it): keep it. Build and test only on the Linux bench JOB.md names, never the working Mac.
STEP 2. Red first: build the planted binary DONE-WHEN names at BASE and show it still passes (the vacuous check); keep that line.
STEP 3. Make it pass in the files PATHS names, citing the SPEC section or the oracle file from each change that implements the rule.
STEP 4. Run the gate: every DONE-WHEN command, and read the last line of each. When a test fails, name its file and say whether your work changed it (yours) or it is unchanged (already red at BASE: run the same test on the unchanged base to say so), and report that line first.
STEP 5. Commit on your own branch with the trailer. Nothing reaches the forge from inside the wall: in the job the git shim records a push, the pull request is the finish JOB.md names, and the member makes both, against the BASE: line's branch, from outside the wall when the card finishes. The pull request body states the diff stat, the tests with what each pins, and what was not done.
