# make tables-block-zero-cost runs the C emitter in its block-less arm

OWNER RULE (Glenn 2026-10-18): \"We do all features across all languages.\" and (2026-10-15) golang is first; this card changes no emitter, runtime or spec, only the Makefile wiring that lets the C arm of an existing gate run.

THE TASK. `make tables-block-zero-cost` regenerates the whole table corpus from a block-less emitter (BLOCKLESS_SCHEMA) into $(BLOCKLESS_ROOT)/{cpp,cs,c} and compares each against the ordinary corpus byte for byte. The C arm calls two macros. Make both exist, where the cpp and cs macros live in the same file family (tables_generate in the Makefile, tables_generate_cs in the C# make file (read) ), taking (generator binary, output root) and listing the same schema files the ordinary C stamps generate (build/tables-generated-c/.stamp and build/tables-generated-c/collections.stamp), so the ordinary corpus and the block-less corpus are produced from ONE list per language and cannot drift (the comment above tables_generate_c in make/c.mk says why). Make the ordinary stamps call the macros, not a second copy of the lines.
STEP 1 decides the shape: run `make -pn tables-block-zero-cost | grep -n 'define tables_generate_c'` (or grep Makefile and make/*.mk for both names) at BASE. (a) Both macros are defined and the target runs: the card's finding is that the gap is not at this BASE; then run the gate, and if it is green say so and finish not-done with the evidence (a card that finds nothing to fix is a welcome report); if it is red for another reason (the C comparison says \"did not write\", the Table.h glob matches nothing, the C out directory is not the one the macro writes), fix that wiring, which is the same defect in another form, and name the cause. (b) A macro is missing: define it in make/c.mk beside the stamp that uses it, listing exactly the generate lines the stamp has.
The C comparison must be able to fail: after the fix run the negative control the Makefile already holds for the byte comparison (tables-block-zero-cost-negative-control) and show it red; never claim the gate green without having seen the C arm compare at least the file count its tables_byte_identical call demands (60).

DONE-WHEN: (1) at BASE, the grep above and the exit status of `make tables-block-zero-cost` are quoted as the red (or, for case (a) green, as the finding); (2) at head `make tables-block-zero-cost` exits 0 and its last lines include `block zero-cost gate: c <n> Table sources byte-identical to the block-less emitter` with n at least 60, and the C sources are byte-identical to the block-less emitter's (the gate's own comparison, which is the proof); (3) `make tables-block-zero-cost-negative-control` still exits 0 (it passes by going red inside); (4) `git diff --stat` against BASE shows only Makefile and make/c.mk, with the generate lines moved, not duplicated; (5) `make tables-c` and `make generated-current` are green, or their only reds are named as present at BASE by running the same target on the unchanged base.

STEP 1. cd into the staged checkout JOB.md names and run git log --oneline -1. Export GOFLAGS=-mod=readonly NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1 before any go command; GOCACHE is already set to the machine's shared build cache (JOB.md names it): keep it. Build and test only on the Linux bench JOB.md names. Run the grep and the make target above at BASE and keep the lines.
STEP 2. Make the smallest change in the files PATHS names.
STEP 3. Run every DONE-WHEN command; read the last line of each. When a target fails, say whether your work changed the cause (run it at BASE to say so), and report that line first.
STEP 4. Commit on your own branch as `schema-c-tables-generate-c-macro-defined: <what>` with the trailer. The report states the diff stat, the red lines, which case (a) or (b) held, and what was not done.

