# C# checked arithmetic over the conformance tests (cs port of schema)

THE LENS. Schema generates the same algorithm in many languages, so every language's strictest checker, run over the generated output for the schema repository's test-schema corpus (every schema under the corpus directories the Makefile's generate targets read, found with grep -n in the Makefile and make/*.mk; do not invent a second corpus), is a separate lens on bugs (Glenn, 2026-10-18; ideas roadmap group schema-per-language-lenses). THIS CARD: CheckForOverflowUnderflow (checked arithmetic) enabled in the generated C# and the runtime, run through the conformance tests, so overflow bugs become exceptions over the generated cs output (readers and writers, and the runtime where the port has one).

Files you may touch: the PATHS above, plus the NEW files the card needs (the card's own make file make/schema-cs-conformance-checked-overflow.mk (the Makefile includes make/*.mk, so no Makefile edit is needed), its own workflow .github/workflows/schema-cs-conformance-checked-overflow.yml (a schedule and workflow_dispatch trigger for nightly parts, push or pull_request for fast parts), and test files; cards share no workflow or Makefile edits so they land independently. When the work needs a file outside PATHS (the port's test tree, generator, fixtures, saved corpus), report PATHS-PROPOSED with the paths and stop that part as not-done). Do not add needs on other cards: this lens stands alone. Worktree: the staged checkout JOB.md names; branch: the card's own; base: sprint/schema-cs.
Libraries considered: the Go standard library and the repository's own tools (tools/negativecontrols, tools/slowgatescan, internal/ci) for gates and tests; the checker or fuzzer named above for the analysis itself, installed fleet-wide from its official source; no hand-written reimplementation of any checker.

DONE-WHEN: the Makefile target exists and passes on the corpus at the card's head; every real finding is fixed with a test that was red at BASE (the failing lines kept); every suppression has a justification; the planted-bad-pattern test is red when the plant is in and green when it is out; CI stays inside its one-minute budget (heavy parts are nightly); the report lists the triage table (finding, REAL or NOT, action) and the other ports' generators that share a flagged pattern.

ROUTE NOTE (coordinator, 2026-10-19). Start from the CARRY head: the scope repair is done there (make/cs.mk and the two .csproj files restored to base; the checked build is a global MSBuild property in the card-owned make fragment) and the negative control is registered in make/negative-controls.json, which is in PATHS and SHARED with other gate cards: add only this card's entry. Attempt 1 held only because no Linux dotnet gate ran. On a Linux member with the pinned dotnet, at the exact head and with no source change unless a gate goes red, run: make tables-cs-checked-overflow-conformance; make tables-cs-checked-overflow-negative-control; go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/ci -run TestCsConformanceRunsUnderCheckedArithmetic; go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./tools/negativecontrols. A real OverflowException in the corpus or runtime is a finding: triage it in the report, never suppress it. If the load guard defers a step, wait for the load to fall and run it again in this attempt; record every command, its exit code and raw log path.

