# C#: cs-cook's hash and checksum arithmetic wraps on purpose, under unchecked

THE GAP. schema-cs-conformance-checked-overflow-t now builds every assembly checked, and that run hit real System.OverflowException in test/cs-cook/src/Walk.cs:297, Header.Fnv1a64: FNV-1a 64 relies on wrapping multiplication, which a checked build refuses. The raw log is the w3 attempt's conformance log on hetzner2 (01-conformance.log of schema-cs-conformance-checked-overflow-t.w3).

THE TASK. Wrap the arithmetic of Header.Fnv1a64, and of every other hash or checksum routine in test/cs-cook that relies on wrapping arithmetic (grep the cs-cook tree for the multiply/add loops of each), in an explicit `unchecked { }` block with a one-line comment saying why (the algorithm is defined modulo 2^64). Wrap only the arithmetic that must wrap; a size or index computation stays checked. Add a check that pins the published FNV-1a 64 test vectors (the empty string = 0xcbf29ce484222325, \"a\" = 0xaf63dc4c8601ec8c, \"foobar\" = 0x85944171f73967e8) under a checked build.

DONE-WHEN: (1) red at BASE: a checked build of cs-cook running the FNV-1a 64 vector check throws OverflowException (keep the line); (2) at head the same checked build passes the three vectors and an unchecked build gives the same values; (3) the report lists every routine wrapped, each with file:line and why it must wrap, and the routines looked at and left checked; (4) go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/ci/ green or its reds named as present at BASE.

STEP 1. cd into the staged checkout JOB.md names and run git log --oneline -1; it is a NEW worktree on the branch this card names. Export GOFLAGS=-mod=readonly NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1 before any go command; GOCACHE is already set to the machine's shared build cache (JOB.md names it): keep it; use the pinned dotnet the Linux bench carries. Build and test only on the Linux bench JOB.md names, never the working Mac.
STEP 2. Red first: run the RED command DONE-WHEN names at BASE and keep its failing line (`no tests to run` before a new test file exists is not the red: write the test, then run it at BASE and keep its failing assertion).
STEP 3. Make it pass in the files PATHS names, citing the SPEC section or the oracle file from each change that implements the rule.
STEP 4. Run the gate: every DONE-WHEN command, and read the last line of each. When a test fails, name its file and say whether your work changed it (yours) or it is unchanged (already red at BASE: run the same test on the unchanged base to say so), and report that line first.
STEP 5. Commit on your own branch with the trailer. Nothing reaches the forge from inside the wall: in the job the git shim records a push, the pull request is the finish JOB.md names, and the member makes both, against the BASE: line's branch, from outside the wall when the card finishes. The pull request body states the diff stat, the tests with what each pins, and what was not done.
