Source. schema-go-testgen-fuzz-target-per-table (head 76facf0f3e7f, 2026-10-18) admits, per input, a re-encoding SHORTER than the input that reads back silently and re-encodes to itself, because a field written at its default is elided and a repeated id lets the last occurrence win. Its cold reader showed the blind spot: a reader that turns a damaged field into its default passes, since the shorter re-encode is accepted. Its report also proposed, outside its PATHS, that cmd/schema/main.go's testgen help paragraph mention the fuzz targets; that line is in this card.

RED AT BASE (sprint/schema-go once schema-go-testgen-fuzz-target-per-table has landed; carded 2026-10-18 from its head 76facf0f3e7f):
- the wire and text targets accept any shorter re-encode that reads back silently: a generated reader edited so that one field's read stores the field's default instead of the decoded value passes every Fuzz<Table>Wire target.
- the testgen help paragraph in cmd/schema/main.go names the round-trip and damage tests only.
- `SCHEMA_SLOW=1 go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/gotable/ -run '^TestGoTestgenFuzzNormalisedInput$' -v` prints `[no tests to run]` (the test is new).

THE TASK. The rule (written into docs/SPEC-TABLES.md (read) 16.11 by the sibling piece schema-go-fuzz-compare-normalised-input-spec, running in parallel; where it has not landed at your BASE, implement it exactly as stated here and cite 16.11): 16.11 replaces the \"shorter re-encoding\" outcome: for an input the reader accepts with a silent report, the re-encoding must equal the NORMALISED input, byte for byte, where the normalised input is the input's own field entries (section 3 framing: reference, kind, payload) with every entry whose payload is the field's declared default dropped, every repeated id but its last occurrence dropped, and the rest in the order the writer writes; the normaliser walks the wire's framing alone and never calls the generated reader. Name the input classes that cannot be normalised (framing damage, a refusal) as outside the comparison. Add the USAGE line. Go: testgen emits that normaliser as a test helper in the generated test file and the Wire and Text targets compare against it; the Json target is named in the report as compared or not, with why. The standard, USAGE.md and cmd/schema/main.go (read) are the sibling's; this piece does not edit them.

THE TESTS, opening with t.Parallel(), no real time:
- TestGoTestgenFuzzNormalisedInput, internal/codegen/gotable/testgenfuzznormal_test.go: testgen of tables/examples in a t.TempDir() runs every target's seed corpus and passes; a generated Patrol reader edited by the test so that one field's read (speed) stores its default instead of the value makes FuzzPatrolWire fail (that edit passes at BASE: the red); the normaliser drops a default-valued entry and keeps the last of a repeated id, each pinned on a hand-built Patrol wire.

DONE-WHEN: `SCHEMA_SLOW=1 go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/gotable/ -run '^(TestGoTestgenFuzzNormalisedInput|TestGoTestgenFuzzTargets)$' -v` RUN and PASS at head (red at BASE: no tests to run, and the edited reader passing the targets). The STEP 5 gate is green or its only reds are named as present at BASE; STEP 5b shows zero differences.

