# A planted fault that drops the sign extension in gotable's Int128From64 left the whole suite green: no test widens a negative int64 to int128

Source. The coordinator's mutation probe: removing the sign extension from Int128From64 (a negative int64 then widens with Hi = 0 instead of all ones) keeps every test green, SCHEMA_SLOW=1 and the serialize.go sibling present included. No test reaches a negative int64 widened to int128 through the generated Go path or the tabletext path.

THE TASK (tests; the code is right and is not changed unless a test finds a real defect, which is then reported with its input and fixed in the smallest way). Add the tests below, each opening with t.Parallel(), no real time:
- TestGoInt128From64SignExtends, internal/codegen/gotable/int128from64_test.go: through runGenerated (the house pattern) with a unit that has an int128 field and an int64 source: widen -1, math.MinInt64 and a value in between (-4294967301) and assert Hi is all ones (0xffffffffffffffff) and Lo is the int64's bits; widen 0, 1 and math.MaxInt64 and assert Hi is 0; assert the widened value converts back to the same int64.
- The generated Go path: a table with an int128 field set from a negative int64 through the generated setter/constructor the unit has, Save then Load, the value (Hi and Lo) is preserved for those three values.
- The tabletext path: the text reader of an int128 field given the decimal text of those three negative values yields Hi all ones and the same Lo; the text writer prints them back as the same decimal text; a positive value stays Hi 0.
- A byte-equal round trip against the C++ wire: the Go Save bytes of a table holding those negative int128 values equal the bytes the C++ writer emits for the same values, compared as a committed fixture under testdata/wire/tables (a new fixture written by the C++ tables suite's writer on the bench, not by hand), and the Go Load of the C++ bytes gives the same Hi and Lo. If the C++ suite has such a fixture already, name it and reuse it.
- Reversed witness: in the same test file, plant the fault (the generated or runtime Int128From64 text with the sign extension dropped, done as a text edit of the generated source in the test's t.TempDir()) and assert every group above goes red under it; the genuine code is green. Check every helper's returned error (internal/ci TestGeneratedHelperResultsAreChecked).
- If Int128From64 has a sibling for the unsigned or the 32-bit widening, test its edge too and say so; do not widen the scope further.

AUDIT. Grep the other ports' int64-to-int128 widening (C, C++, C#, Java, JS, Dart, Elixir, Lua, Luau, Squirrel, Rust) and list each in the finish as REAL (a widening that does not sign-extend, or is not tested for a negative input, with file and line and the input that shows it) or NOT (sign-extends and is tested). A REAL one is a proposed card; do not edit another language's code.

DONE-WHEN: `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/gotable/ -run '^TestGoInt128From64SignExtends$' -v` RUNs and PASSes at head, and the plant turns it red (the failing line kept); the C++ tables make target exits 0 with the new fixture; `SCHEMA_SLOW=1 go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/gotable/ ./internal/ci/` is green or its only reds are named as present at BASE; the audit list is in the finish.
