# The JSON form's bytes(N) read refuses non-canonical base64 by name; writers emit canonical base64 only

OWNER RULE (Glenn, 2026-10-15 12:34Z): \"When you improve schema, improve the standard, and then fix it in golang and verify the fix. Stop the multi-language stuff until we have finished our dogfooding in golang.\" The rule is written in docs/SPEC-TABLES.md first, in a commit of its own, and Go is then made to match it; no other language's emitter is edited. This card is the JSON twin of the landed card schema-go-text-base64-canonical, which defines the refusal name and the canonical rule; read that card's section of the standard first and reuse it, never restate a second rule.

SCOPE (Glenn, 2026-10-17): card all the schema Go improvements so schema Go reaches 100% before any port; this card is one of them.

Ruling (coordinator, 2026-10-18, applying Glenn's \"write strictly; forgive on read only if near-free and never at any security cost\"): the strict JSON read refuses non-canonical base64 by name, `base64_not_canonical`, the same name and the same canonical rule as the text card; the tolerant JSON read accepts it and reports it; writers emit canonical base64 only.

DEFECT. The JSON form's bytes(N) read is lenient about base64. readBase64 in internal/tabletext/read.go and the generated walk in internal/codegen/gotable/json.go (near line 2010) skip `=` anywhere and ignore the pad bits. So `AQAAAAAAAAAAAB==` (non-zero pad bits), `=` in the middle, embedded whitespace, and a missing or extra `=` are all read without complaint.

RED AT BASE: a unit test through runGenerated (below) finds no refusal for each of those inputs in the strict read, and no report in the tolerant read; `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/gotable/ -run '^TestGoJSONReadRefusesNonCanonicalBase64$' -v` prints `[no tests to run]` at BASE (the test is new).

NOTE. Pass 4 carries head 4ee3fb4b: the code is right. (1) Merge current sprint/schema-go; in docs/SPEC-TABLES.md keep base's wstring(N) row and this card's bytes(N) row. (2) Replace the 311bb83a paragraph (around lines 15474-15483) that claims a different padding grammar with a pointer to the bytes(N) row and §16.8, and fix the stale doc comment on TestTextBase64PaddingIsTheWalks. (3) The tabletext allocation test must not re-run its own test binary as a child process: unit tests spawn no programs. Measure allocations in-process with testing.AllocsPerRun, or move it to a functional-tag file. Fix the literal \\n in its Fatalf. (4) A raw newline inside a fixed bytes(N) JSON string must give the same refusal as *bytes (malformed).

NOTE (attempt 7). Merge the base tip first. Rebuild the canonical check on top of 157a5c6d's unescaped body: both the check pass and the decode pass run over the unescaped bytes. Replace the child-process allocation test (exec.Command(os.Args[0]) in internal/tabletext/text_test.go:323; unit tests spawn no programs) with in-process testing.AllocsPerRun, or move it to a functional-tag file. Fix the literal \\\\n in its Fatalf. Regenerate generated/bench. Probes: \"\\/w==\" accepted, non-zero pad bits refused, = in the middle refused.

THE TASK.
(1) Standard, its own commit first: in docs/SPEC-TABLES.md 16.2 (and 16.8 where it names the carrier), the bytes(N) body of the JSON form is canonical base64 by the text card's rule. The strict read refuses a body that decodes but is not canonical (non-zero pad bits, `=` anywhere but the end, any byte outside the alphabet and `=` including whitespace, a missing or extra `=`) by `base64_not_canonical`; a body that does not decode at all keeps the refusal it has today. The tolerant read accepts a non-canonical body and reports it as the standard already reports a tolerated defect. Writers emit canonical base64 only. Add the one-line USAGE entry.
(2) Go: in readBase64 (internal/tabletext/read.go) and the generated walk (internal/codegen/gotable/json.go), decide canonical the same way the text card does (decode with base64.StdEncoding.Strict(), refuse unless len(body) == base64.StdEncoding.EncodedLen(len(decoded))), strict read refusing with TableRefuseBase64NotCanonical (landed by the text card, reuse it), tolerant read accepting and reporting. Pin the writer: SaveJSON already writes canonical text; assert it in a test.
(3) Regenerate the generated files with the repo's make targets; never hand-edit a generated file. tables/goruntime/goruntime.go and generated/bench/** change only if the emitter does; commit what the make writes. The internal/ci tree-current checks must pass.

THE TEST, opening with t.Parallel(), no real time, no sockets: TestGoJSONReadRefusesNonCanonicalBase64 in internal/codegen/gotable/jsonbase64canonical_test.go, through runGenerated with tables/examples/Tables.schema (read), for a table with a bytes(N) field and for one variable table: for each of non-zero pad bits, `=` in the middle, embedded whitespace, a missing `=`, an extra `=`: the strict read refuses with errors.Is(err, TableRefuseBase64NotCanonical) and reason text `base64_not_canonical`; the tolerant read accepts it and reports it; a canonical input reads clean and round-trips to the same JSON. A body that is not base64 at all keeps its existing refusal.

DONE-WHEN: the test above RUNs and PASSes at head (red at BASE: no tests to run, and the lenient reads above); it covers both readers (strict and tolerant) for non-zero pad bits, `=` in the middle, embedded whitespace, and a missing or extra `=`; a canonical input round-trips. The tree gate passes (STEP 5), or its only reds are named as present at BASE.

STEP 1. cd into the staged checkout JOB.md names and run git log --oneline -1; it is a NEW worktree on the branch this card names. Record BASE_SHA=$(git rev-parse HEAD). Export GOFLAGS='-mod=readonly -p=4' NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1 before any go command; GOCACHE is already the machine's shared build cache: keep it. Build and test only on the Linux bench JOB.md names, never the working Mac.
STEP 2. The standard first: write the rule into docs/SPEC-TABLES.md and the USAGE entry; commit that alone, before any code.
