# The semantic-equal oracle for JSON texts, by SPEC-TABLES 16.12 (pro)

GOAL (Glenn, 2026-10-17): \"If we can match everything there, then we have a very flexible binary variable table format.\" Every JSONTestSuite file the policy accepts (all y_, plus the i_ files 16.11 rules accept) loads into a binary VARIABLE table with no hand-written schema per file, and comes back out equal in value.

SOURCE-SPLIT: split (2026-10-18) of schema-go-json-any-in-variable-table, which keeps the 16.12 design ruling; this card builds the oracle that ruling specifies (Glenn 2026-10-18: \"decompose work into pro level chunks, as much as possible\"). It needs schema-go-json-any-in-variable-table (the number-equality rule lives in 16.12) and schema-go-jsontestsuite-policy (internal/jsontestsuite and its Load).

OWNER RULE (Glenn, 2026-10-15 12:34Z): \"When you improve schema, improve the standard, and then fix it in golang and verify the fix. Stop the multi-language stuff until we have finished our dogfooding in golang.\" Where this card changes a rule, the rule is written in docs/SPEC-TABLES.md first, in a commit of its own, and Go is then made to match it; no other language's emitter is edited.

SOURCE (Glenn, 2026-10-17): schema adopts JSONTestSuite (github.com/nst/JSONTestSuite, MIT) as the outside oracle for its JSON text form. His rule for it: \"write strictly; read forgivingly only where that is nearly free in performance; never lose any security; security trumps.\"

RED AT BASE:
- `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/jsontestsuite/ -run '^TestJsonSemanticEqual$' -v` prints `testing: warning: no tests to run` or `[setup failed]` (the test is new; the package is the policy card's).
- internal/jsontestsuite/equal.go does not exist.

THE TASK. Read docs/SPEC-TABLES.md (read) section 16.12 at your BASE first; if it is absent, stop with Verdict: HOLD naming the missing section. Implement its rule exactly; where 16.12 is silent on a case below, follow this card and name the gap in the report.
(2) THE ORACLE, internal/jsontestsuite/equal.go (standard library only): Equal(a, b []byte) (bool, string) compares two JSON texts by 16.12's rule and returns where they first differ. It is bounded: depth 128, a number compared without expanding it (1e999999999 compares in constant memory), no allocation proportional to anything but the text. It cites 16.12 from the function.
(3) THE TEST, internal/jsontestsuite/equal_test.go, TestJsonSemanticEqual, opening with t.Parallel(): over every file Load calls accept, Equal(file, file) holds; Equal holds against a rewrite that changes only whitespace and spells one ASCII character of a string as its \\u escape; REVERSED WITNESS: for each accepted file holding a number, a string or an object with two keys, the mutated text (one digit changed; one character of a string changed; the first two keys swapped) is NOT Equal, and the reason names the place. Plus `[1e999999999]` against `[1e999999998]`, compared within one AllocsPerRun-bounded call.

DONE-WHEN: `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/jsontestsuite/ -run '^TestJsonSemanticEqual' -v` RUN and PASS at head, with the AllocsPerRun check named in the output (the -run is a prefix so it selects TestJsonSemanticEqual and any TestJsonSemanticEqualAllocs) (red at BASE: no tests to run or setup failed); `go vet ./internal/jsontestsuite/` is clean; the STEP 5 gate is green or its only reds are named as present at BASE.

FINDINGS TO FIX. A cold read of the carried head 712859d4b3c7bdfc08ba426265a999d14af8d8f9 (branch sprint/schema-go-json-semantic-equal-oracle.w2.g1.e21) found these; fix each, red test first, and pin each probe:
(a) SPEC-TABLES 16.13 says Equal refuses if either text is refused. But the walk stops at the first difference, so a malformed text is reported as a difference instead of a refusal:
  - `Equal([1],[01])` returns \"number 1 != 0\";
  - `Equal([1,\"\\uD800\"],[2,\"x\"])` returns \"number 1 != 2\", hiding the lone surrogate;
  - `[1,,]` vs `[1]`, `[,]` vs `[]` and `[true]` vs `[truex]` each return \"array length differs\".
  Fix: validate each text fully first (a bounded pass, depth 128, no allocation proportional to anything but the text), or read both texts to the end after the first difference and refuse if either is malformed. Pin each probe above as a refusal (never reported as a difference), and keep the reversed witnesses green.
(b) In eqArray and eqObject the labels are swapped: `!commaA` reports \"in b\" and `!commaB` reports \"in a\", so `Equal([1,2],[1 2])` says \"expected ',' in a\" when the malformed text is b. Swap them and pin `Equal([1,2],[1 2])` and its mirror `Equal([1 2],[1,2])` to name the right side, for both arrays and objects.
(c) The AllocsPerRun check for `[1e999999999]` against `[1e999999998]` must be selected by DONE-WHEN's -run (it is, above): keep it in TestJsonSemanticEqual or in a TestJsonSemanticEqualAllocs, opening with t.Parallel(), and confirm the PASS line names it.

