# A strict JSON read accepts an unknown key and reports it by key path, as the binary form tolerates an unknown wire id

OWNER RULE (Glenn, 2026-10-15 12:34Z): \"When you improve schema, improve the standard, and then fix it in golang and verify the fix. Stop the multi-language stuff until we have finished our dogfooding in golang.\" Where this card changes a rule, the rule is written in docs/SPEC-TABLES.md first, in a commit of its own, and Go is then made to match it; no other language's emitter is edited.

Source. The schema Go fuzz campaign (Glenn, 2026-10-17: \"Should we try to fuzz test golang schema implementation more? I'd like to catch anything else NOW vs. after ports.\"), random schemas generated, compiled and driven through every Go read and write path on hetzner and hulk; this card is one minimized finding, confirmed by hand.

RE-SCOPED (coordinator, 2026-10-17): Glenn's accepted 10-17 strict-read ruling says the plain read accepts unknown fields and reports them; it refuses only on integrity, bounds, kind, framing, form, build version or a missing required field. This card therefore does not refuse unknown keys and adds no table marker, no refusal reason and no central refusal: it states the policy for the JSON text form and makes each unknown key visible in the report by its key path. Heavy because the standard text is a ruling: schema-go-strict-read-by-default's brief never mentions unknown keys, so the JSON policy is not yet written down or pinned.

RED AT BASE (sprint/schema-go @ 98b8c22d3e98, hetzner2, 2026-10-17). Unit `table Root { at timestamp }`: RootFromJson of {\"bt\":\"2026-10-17T12:34:05Z\"} (one byte of the key flipped) returns ok=true with Unknown 1 and report.Err() nil, the field left at its default, and the report does not say which key was unknown (sprint/schema-go @ 0592fd845, hulk). In real use (the nova notice read, coordinator, 2026-10-17) one flipped byte in a key such as \"challenge\" became \"chbllenge\" and emptied a whole nested record with nothing in the report naming it. `SCHEMA_SLOW=1 go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/gotable/ -run '^TestGoStrictJsonAcceptsAndReportsUnknownKeys$' -v` prints `testing: warning: no tests to run` and `ok  	github.com/mas-bandwidth/schema/v2/internal/codegen/gotable	0.003s [no tests to run]` (new).

THE TASK. Builds on schema-go-strict-read-by-default (the strict default and the named tolerant read). (1) Standard, its own commit first: docs/SPEC-TABLES.md 16.2 (and 6.5 where it touches) states that every strict JSON entry point accepts an unknown key, counts it in the report and names its key path, and does not refuse it; say how this matches section 4's wire tolerance (an unknown wire id is tolerated and counted) and that a misspelt key is therefore visible in the report, not refused. Spell the key path for a nested table, an array element and a map value, and say how many paths the report keeps (a bound, with the count still exact past it). The short docs/USAGE.md entry says how a caller reads them. (2) Go: the report names the key path of each unknown key (a new TableReport member, kept before LayoutHash so R23 holds, filled by json.go and jsongraph.go through nested reads); Unknown keeps its count; report.Err() stays nil for unknown keys; no new refusal. No other emitter edited.

THE TEST, internal/codegen/gotable/unknownkey_test.go, TestGoStrictJsonAcceptsAndReportsUnknownKeys, opening with t.Parallel(), through runGenerated: a table and a nested table; a top-level flipped key ({\"bt\":...}) and a flipped nested key each read ok at every strict entry point, Unknown counted, the key path named in the report, report.Err() nil; the tolerant read reports the same; past the path bound the count stays exact; a table without unknown keys reads as at BASE with an empty path list.

DONE-WHEN: `SCHEMA_SLOW=1 go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/gotable/ -run '^TestGoStrictJsonAcceptsAndReportsUnknownKeys$' -v` RUN and PASS at head, the standard commit is first, and the STEP 5 gate is green or its only reds are named as present at BASE.

