WHAT IS THERE: docs/SPEC-TABLES.md section 2.13 (line ~4004, \"Text lists\") holds the text-list construct; line ~4072 says the fixed, block and message forms apply only to fixed tables; line ~4196 says \"Go carries the construct first, and every other target refuses it by name, centrally, at generation\". compiler/tablesstringarrays.go holds refuseTextForms, the central cross-target refusal of the text-list constructs. internal/codegen/gotable/message.go and messageregion.go emit the message batches and the announcement (tableMessageCoreTypes, tableMessageRuntime, messageSlot); refusal.go holds the emitter's named refusals.

# The message form is emitted for a variable table holding a text list, and the unit's own announcement is then refused (element kind 12)

RULING (the coordinator, 2026-10-19): SPEC-TABLES §2.13 holds: the message form is INAPPLICABLE to text lists (`[..N]string(M)` and `[..N]?string(M)`, and the unbounded spellings with them). Today Go (and C++) still emit message batches for a variable table containing a text list, and both announcement readers then refuse element kind 12, so the unit's own announcement is refused. Context: cpp-carries-bounded-text-lists attempt w3, head 24bb2379, met it on the C++ side.

THE TASK. (1) The standard first, in its own commit: in docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §2.13 state that the message form is inapplicable to a table carrying a text list and is refused by name at generation, and replace the backend sentence of §2.13 with exactly: \"Go carries every spelling; C++ carries the bounded [..N] spellings; every other target refuses by name.\" Add the one-line docs/USAGE.md entry. (2) Go: at generate time, the message form is refused by name for a table carrying a text list: the Go emitter emits no message batch, no vocabulary entry and no announcement entry for such a table, and the refusal names the form, the table and the field (for example `the message form is inapplicable to table <T>: field <f> is a text list (docs/SPEC-TABLES.md §2.13)`). Use the EXISTING refusal path the generator already uses for an inapplicable form (search the compiler and internal/codegen/gotable for how a form that does not apply to a table is refused or left out, and reuse it); write no second mechanism. A unit whose other tables are message-eligible keeps their batches, and its announcement reads back clean. Cite §2.13 from the changed function. (3) Regenerate the Go trees: `make generated/bench/tables/go/.stamp generated/bench/paired/go/.stamp generated/bench/go/.stamp` (internal/ci TestGoBenchTreesAreCurrent checks them) and commit what they write. (4) C++ is NOT edited here: name the C++ follow-on (cpptable must refuse the message form for a table with a bounded text list the same way) in the report as a proposed card.

THE TESTS, each opening with t.Parallel(), no real time:
- TestGoMessageFormRefusesTextLists, internal/codegen/gotable/messagetextlist_test.go, pure Go over Generate's output (no compile): a unit with table A holding `rules [..16]string(64)`, table B holding `notes [..4]?string(8)`, and table C with only int32 fields: the generated Go holds no message batch, vocabulary or announcement entry for A or B, holds C's, and the refusal is reported naming A and its field and B and its field. Red at BASE (A's and B's batches are emitted).
- REVERSED WITNESS in the same file: TestGoMessageAnnouncementReadsBackWithTextListTable, through runGenerated (behind slowtest.Gate): the same unit's own Announce is read by AnnounceRead with a clean report (at BASE it is refused on element kind 12).

DONE-WHEN: `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/gotable/ -run '^TestGoMessageFormRefusesTextLists$' -v` RUNs and PASSes at head (red at BASE); `SCHEMA_SLOW=1 go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/gotable/ -run '^TestGoMessageAnnouncementReadsBackWithTextListTable$' -v` PASSes at head and fails at BASE; `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./compiler/ ./internal/ci/` is green or its only reds are named as present at BASE; the §2.13 sentence reads exactly as THE TASK (1) gives it.

STEP 1. cd into the staged checkout JOB.md names and run git log --oneline -1; it is a NEW worktree on the branch this card names. Record BASE_SHA=$(git rev-parse HEAD). Export GOFLAGS='-mod=readonly -p=4' NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1 before any go command; GOCACHE is already set to the machine's shared build cache (JOB.md names it): keep it. Build and test only on the Linux bench JOB.md names, never the working Mac.
STEP 2. Red first: write the tests TEST and THE TASK name, each opening with t.Parallel(); run each and keep its failing line.
STEP 3. Make them pass in the files PATHS names, citing the section of the standard from each function that implements a rule.
STEP 4. Run the gate: the go test lines of DONE-WHEN, then go test -count=1 -timeout 600s on the other packages DONE-WHEN names, and read the last line of each. When a test fails, name its file and say whether that file was changed by your work (yours) or is unchanged (already red at BASE: run the same test on the unchanged base to say so), and report that line first.
STEP 5. Commit on your own branch with the trailer. Nothing reaches the forge from inside the wall: in the job the git shim records a push, the pull request is the finish JOB.md names (the last STEP), and the member makes both, against the BASE: line's branch, from outside the wall when the card finishes. The pull request body states the diff stat, the tests with what each pins, and what was not done.
STEP 6. End as JOB.md says: where JOB.md ends the card with its pull request, that is the end and there is nothing else to write, the gate's lines in the pull request body; where it asks for RESULT.md, write it in JOB.md's shape (head, branch, verdict, gate, output, report). The report starts with Verdict: LAND or Verdict: HOLD on line 1 and Head: <sha> on line 2.

RESOURCE RULES (the bench sandbox caps a job at 256 processes and 8 GiB). Every package-wide go command and make target runs under nice with -p 4 (the GOFLAGS export carries -p=4), one at a time: never two at once, never in the background. A test red at head is rerun at the unchanged BASE only for its own package, never the whole tree at BASE. Commit the work before running the internal/ci gate. Write no scratch test files. Never on amd-2 or amd-3: if JOB.md stages the card on either, stop at once with not-done naming the bench, before any build.

RULES.
