# nightly fuzzing (go port of schema)

NIGHTLY FUZZING (Glenn, 2026-10-18). Fuzz the go port's generated readers with its native coverage-guided fuzzer: native go test -fuzz. Targets: load, strict read and text/JSON parse for every table in a fuzz corpus schema set (pick the set from the schema repository's test-schema corpus (every schema under the corpus directories the Makefile's generate targets read, found with grep -n in the Makefile and make/*.mk; do not invent a second corpus) so that every table shape is covered, and list it in a file the harness reads). Seed the fuzzer from the Go fuzz corpus (internal/fuzz/testdata and the go test -fuzz seeds) and the conformance vectors. Extend the existing per-table Go fuzz targets (internal/fuzz) so every table in the fuzz corpus set has load, strict-read and text/JSON-parse targets, run nightly with -fuzztime.

Files you may touch: the PATHS above, plus the NEW files the card needs (the card's own make file make/schema-go-nightly-fuzz.mk (the Makefile includes make/*.mk, so no Makefile edit is needed), its own workflow .github/workflows/schema-go-nightly-fuzz.yml (a schedule and workflow_dispatch trigger for nightly parts, push or pull_request for fast parts), and test files; cards share no workflow or Makefile edits so they land independently. When the work needs a file outside PATHS (the port's test tree, generator, fixtures, saved corpus), report PATHS-PROPOSED with the paths and stop that part as not-done). Do not add needs on other cards: this lens stands alone. Worktree: the staged checkout JOB.md names; branch: the card's own; base: sprint/schema-go.
Libraries considered: the Go standard library and the repository's own tools (tools/negativecontrols, tools/slowgatescan, internal/ci) for gates and tests; the checker or fuzzer named above for the analysis itself, installed fleet-wide from its official source; no hand-written reimplementation of any checker.

DONE-WHEN: the Makefile target exists and passes on the corpus at the card's head; every real finding is fixed with a test that was red at BASE (the failing lines kept); every suppression has a justification; the planted-bad-pattern test is red when the plant is in and green when it is out; CI stays inside its one-minute budget (heavy parts are nightly); the report lists the triage table (finding, REAL or NOT, action) and the other ports' generators that share a flagged pattern.

STEP 1. Enter your worktree with cd <worktree path> && git log --oneline -1; it is a NEW worktree on the branch this card names. Export GOFLAGS=-mod=readonly NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1 before any go command; GOCACHE is already set to the machine's shared build cache (JOB.md names it): keep it. Build and test only on the Linux bench JOB.md names, never the working Mac, and never run the heavy tool on the studio.
STEP 1. INSTALL. Install the tool fleet-wide from its official source (Glenn's standing grant: the fleet admin installs software through the fleet ansible play in rowan-tools/fleet, never by hand on one machine, never from an unofficial mirror). If you cannot reach the play from the wall, make the Makefile target probe for the tool and report 'tool missing: <name>' as not-done, naming the machines lacking it; never fake a pass. Pin the tool's version in the Makefile.
STEP 2. HARNESS. One fuzz entry point per reader kind, generated by the port's emitter or a script in the Makefile, driven by a Makefile target with a time budget variable (default 10 minutes per port). The oracle is: no crash, no hang, no sanitizer/assert finding, and a refusal is a clean typed error, never a panic or silent success on malformed input.
STEP 3. NIGHTLY. Add a scheduled (nightly) trigger to the card's own workflow running the target for the time budget; never per push. Crashes found are minimised and saved as regression inputs committed to this port's corpus directory, and a fast test replays every saved input on every push (no real time: replay only, the clock is an argument).
STEP 4. FINDINGS. Every distinct finding is carded as its own fix: write it in the report as 'FIX-CARD: <slug>: <input file> <one line>' so the coordinator can card it. Do not fix findings in this card unless the fix is one line.
STEP 5. PROOF THE GATE FIRES. Plant a reader bug (for example drop a bounds check in a scratch copy of the generated reader, switched on only by the test) (in a scratch copy of the generated output or through a generator switch that only a test turns on), show the fuzzer finds it inside the nightly time budget and the saved input then fails the replay test, and keep the red line. Follow the repo's negative-control convention (make/negative-controls.json, tools/negativecontrols): register the control there.
STEP 7. Commit on your own branch with the trailer. Nothing reaches the forge from inside the wall: in the job the git shim records a push, the pull request is the finish JOB.md names (the last STEP), and the member makes both, against sprint/schema-go, from outside the wall when the card finishes. The pull request body states the diff stat, the tests with what each pins, and what was not done.
STEP 8. End as JOB.md says (docs/SPEC-CARD-CONTRACT.md): where JOB.md ends the card with its pull request, that is the end and there is nothing else to write, the gate's lines in the pull request body; where it asks for RESULT.md, write it in JOB.md's shape (head, branch, verdict, gate, output, report). The report starts with Verdict: LAND or Verdict: HOLD on line 1 and Head: <sha> on line 2.

RULES.
Work only in the job directory this card names.
Never force-push or rebase a shared branch.
Never kill a process you did not start.
Never start a server on this machine.
No `rm -rf` outside the job directory.
Report what was not done.
No gh, no push: JOB.md's finish does both, from outside the wall. Never merge. Every `go test` gets `-timeout 600s`; every new test opens with `t.Parallel()`.
Unit tests use no real time, no sockets, no network and no outside services: time is an argument and a test clock starts at 1000.0. Fuzzing, analyzers that run for minutes and anything needing the network are functional tier, behind the slow gate (read internal/slowtest), never in a unit test.
Install tools only from official sources and only through the fleet play; never print or store a secret or token.
Make the smallest change that does the task, with no refactor beyond what the task names.
Never read outside the job's working directory; never search /, ~ or /Volumes; search inside the checkout only.
ROADMAP.md and docs/roadmap.sexp are lander-owned ledgers inside every card's PATHS: commit the ROADMAP.md the roadmap tool renders if the card's work moves an item.
A test that SKIPS is not a pass: a gate line must show --- PASS. A red is pre-existing only if reproduced at BASE in the same environment.
Finish LAND with --head <sha>; finish HOLD with --failed. \"Not done\" is a welcome report; a green claim you did not run is not.
ROUTE NOTE: coordinator 2026-10-19: (1) make/go.mk must use `-include tools/schema-go-nightly-fuzz/nightly.mk` (or a $(wildcard) guard), so ./internal/ci (TestWideScalarProbeModuleBoundary copies Makefile and make/ without tools/) stays green; run all of ./internal/ci at head. (2) Every workflow runs on the fleet's self-hosted runners, never GitHub-hosted (Glenn 2026-10-18): the new nightly workflow's runs-on uses the repo's self-hosted fleet labels, not ubuntu-latest. (3) PATHS-PROPOSED names tools/AGENTS.md beside tools/agentsmap/catalog.go.


ROUTE NOTE (rowan, 2026-10-19, rowan r7 BROKEN at 3bd64e9a, recorded late; the harness files are unchanged at attempt 8, so this applies there; receipts in ~/.local/state/nova-reads/schema-go-nightly-fuzz.r7.rowan.1/):
(1) The JSON lexer tokens in jsonLexerTokens (internal/ci/nightlyfuzzgen.go:217) go inside table-shaped JSON, as the value of a real field of each table, not as bare documents. The P2b plant (surrogate lookahead in.pos+2 changed to +1 at maps/MapdemoTableJson.go:1049) must be FOUND BY FUZZING, not by a dictionary seed that already contains the crasher.
(2) Seed every table from the MANIFEST instance lines: testdata/wire/tables/*.bin and testdata/conformance/tables/json/*.json, each prefixed with its own table's selector, and accept .bin in the seed loader. Add a test asserting that populated pointer, list and map shapes are seeded.
(3) Show the P4 plant (pointers/GraphdemoTable.go:1225 Resolve bound) is reachable with a replay input, then found by a bounded fuzz run.")
