# A fixed [N]T array whose wire count is short of N is read silently and re-encoded zero-filled; the strict read refuses it by name

OWNER RULE (Glenn, 2026-10-15 12:34Z): \"When you improve schema, improve the standard, and then fix it in golang and verify the fix. Stop the multi-language stuff until we have finished our dogfooding in golang.\" The rule is written in docs/SPEC-TABLES.md first, in a commit of its own, and Go is then made to match it; no other language's emitter is edited, and every other target refuses a new construct by name, centrally, in compiler/generate.go, as compiler/tablesstringarrays.go does for text lists. Worked example: the landed card schema-string-arrays-and-top-level-array-roots (standard commit, then Go, central refusal, red-then-green tests).

SCOPE (Glenn, 2026-10-17): card all the schema Go improvements so schema Go reaches 100% before any port; this card is one of them.

Source. Found by schema-go-testgen-fuzz-target-per-table (head 15c33bbac6c9a4d3f2e271b3a435ec123dd64c72, PR mas-bandwidth/schema#2129, bench hulk, 2026-10-18; branch sprint/schema-go-testgen-fuzz-target-per-table.w6.g1.e21, finished HOLD on this and one sibling defect, schema-go-reader-enum-variant-ref-not-swapped). Input FuzzGlobalSettingsWire, 61 bytes, prefix 01010802000000020c0161030e0e0a010000803e0000803e0000803e00f3ea...: a [3]float32 body whose array count byte is 0x01 in place of 0x03 (the bytes `0e 0e 0a 01 0000803e ...`: kind 14, L 14, element kind 10, count 1) is read as ok with a SILENT report; its trailing 8 bytes of float payload are ignored, and the value re-encodes with count 3 and the missing elements zeroed. So a damaged count lands as a plausible, different value, and the bytes the reader ignored never appear anywhere. The full 61 bytes are reproducible without the PR: build the GlobalSettings sample value, Save it, and write 0x01 over the array's count byte (the byte after the element kind 0a that follows `0e 0e`).

SPEC CONFLICT, to be settled in THE STANDARD FIRST and not guessed. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md section 4 (\"A changed array BOUND\") says a count short of the reader's bound fills what the writer sent and leaves the tail at its declared defaults, silently, and section 18 lists a bounded array made fixed (or the reverse) and a grown bound as PASSING in silence. That is the evolution tolerance, and it is the reason this read is silent today. But a FIXED array is whole on the wire (section 2.3, \"a fixed array whole\"; the writer writes all N elements or none), and the text form already refuses a short fixed array as `text_bound` (section 16.9.4). Ruling to implement (coordinator, 2026-10-18, from the card name): the VARIABLE-wire read of a field declared `[N]T` (fixed, not `[..N]T`) refuses a count that is not N: the strict read (6.5) refuses it as `malformed` naming the field; the tolerant read keeps today's fill, but COUNTS the event as `malformed` naming the field (existing counter, no new one) so it is never silent. A count above N keeps its present rule (clamped). `[..N]T` bounded arrays are UNCHANGED: a short count there stays the silent evolution tolerance. If writing the standard shows that this breaks a row of sections 4 or 18 that the owner has fixed (a `[..N]T` made `[N]T` across a stored file), do NOT widen the change: stop, commit the standard text and the red test only, and report Verdict: HOLD naming the row and the two options (keep the evolution tolerance and fix the harness to normalise this class instead; or refuse and move the protocol id), because that call is the coordinator's.

RED AT BASE (sprint/schema-go @ the tip, 2026-10-18):
- the GlobalSettings wire with the [3]float32 count byte at 0x01 is read by GlobalSettingsLoad as ok with a silent report, and by GlobalSettingsReadStrict as no error (the fuzz target prints the input in hex and the re-encoding with count 3).
- `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/gotable/ -run '^TestGoWireFixedArrayShortCount$' -v` prints `[no tests to run]` (the test is new).

THE TASK. (1) Standard, its own commit first: in docs/SPEC-TABLES.md section 4 state the rule above where the changed-bound row is, naming that it covers `[N]T` and not `[..N]T`, and in section 6.5 that the strict read of a short fixed count is `malformed` (an existing member of the one refusal enum TableRefuseReason; add no member); add the one-line USAGE entry. (2) Go: in internal/codegen/gotable/read.go, where the generated counted read of a FIXED array field decides its count, a count below N sets r.Report.Malformed and names the field in Reason (`malformed: <field>`, as tableTextMalformed spells it, set only when Reason is empty), keeps filling what the writer sent, and leaves the tail at defaults; one compare and one branch per read, no allocation. (3) internal/tablewire/decode.go (the engine reader) does the same so the two readers agree. (4) Regenerate the Go trees and the runtime: `make generated/bench/tables/go/.stamp generated/bench/paired/go/.stamp generated/bench/go/.stamp` (internal/ci TestGoBenchTreesAreCurrent checks them) and keep tables/goruntime/goruntime.go in step if the emitter's output for it changes; commit what they write. The fix is the SMALLEST change in the gotable emitter.

THE TESTS, each opening with t.Parallel(), no real time:
- TestGoWireFixedArrayShortCount, internal/codegen/gotable/wirefixedarraycount_test.go, through runGenerated with the unit that declares GlobalSettings: the crasher (the sample's Save with the count byte set to 0x01, built in the test and also pinned as the literal 61-byte hex once you have the full bytes from the Save) read by the tolerant Load returns the elements the writer sent, Report.Malformed is set and Reason names the field; GlobalSettingsReadStrict of the same bytes refuses with errors.Is(err, TableRefuseMalformed); the same bytes with the count byte 0x03 read clean and re-encode byte for byte; counts 0 and 2 behave as 1 does; a count of 4 keeps today's clamped behaviour. Red at BASE (silent report, no error), green at head. A `[..N]T` field in the same unit given a short count still reads silent (the unchanged side).
- TestEngineWireFixedArrayShortCount, internal/tablewire/wirefixedarraycount_test.go: the same bytes through the engine's Decode report Malformed.

DONE-WHEN: `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/gotable/ -run '^TestGoWireFixedArrayShortCount$' -v` and `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/tablewire/ -run '^TestEngineWireFixedArrayShortCount$' -v` RUN and PASS at head (red at BASE as above). The STEP 5 gate is green or its only reds are named as present at BASE; STEP 5b shows only the intended differences.

STEP 1. cd into the staged checkout JOB.md names and run git log --oneline -1; it is a NEW worktree on the branch this card names. Record BASE_SHA=$(git rev-parse HEAD) for STEP 5b. Export GOFLAGS='-mod=readonly -p=4' NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1 before any go command; GOCACHE is already set to the machine's shared build cache (JOB.md names it): keep it. Build and test only on the Linux bench JOB.md names, never the working Mac.
STEP 2. The standard first: write the rule into docs/SPEC-TABLES.md (the sections THE TASK names; SPEC.md too where it says so) and the short docs/USAGE.md entry, and commit that alone, before any code.
STEP 3. Red first: write the tests TEST and THE TASK name, each opening with t.Parallel(); run each and keep its failing line.
STEP 4. Make them pass in the files PATHS names, citing the section of the standard from each function that implements a rule.
STEP 5. Run the gate: go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/gotable/ ./compiler/ ./internal/check/ ./internal/parser/ ./ir/ ./internal/ci/ ./cmd/schema/ ./internal/tablewire/ and read the last line of each; then make test-go, and make conformance SCHEMA_SKIP_LEGS=cs,java,elixir,dart,js and read the go column (a red in another leg's column is named and compared against BASE, never fixed here), all on the bench. When a test fails, name its file and say whether that file was changed by your work (yours) or is unchanged (already red at BASE: run the same test on the unchanged base to say so), and report that line first.
STEP 5b. GENERATED OUTPUT, base against head. Build two compilers inside the job: mkdir -p .cmp/base; git archive $BASE_SHA | tar -x -C .cmp/base; go build -C .cmp/base -o $PWD/.cmp/schema-base ./cmd/schema; go build -o .cmp/schema-head ./cmd/schema; add .cmp to .git/info/exclude so it is never committed. Then for every file f in git ls-files of *.schema at BASE, and for each of --lang go and --lang cpp, run both binaries as generate --lang L --out .cmp/out/{base,head}/<f with / as _> f, keeping each exit status and stderr; compare them, then diff -rq the two output trees. Report the count of files compared and every difference. INTENDED DIFFERENCES: in --lang go output only, the changed read of the construct in every unit that has it, and nothing else; --lang cpp none. Every other difference is a defect of this card.
STEP 6. Commit on your own branch with the trailer. Nothing reaches the forge from inside the wall: in the job the git shim records a push, the pull request is the finish JOB.md names (the last STEP), and the member makes both, against the BASE: line's branch, from outside the wall when the card finishes. The pull request body states the diff stat, the tests with what each pins, the STEP 5b count and differences, and what was not done.
STEP 7. End as JOB.md says: where JOB.md ends the card with its pull request, that is the end and there is nothing else to write, the gate's lines in the pull request body; where it asks for RESULT.md, write it in JOB.md's shape (head, branch, verdict, gate, output, report). The report starts with Verdict: LAND or Verdict: HOLD on line 1 and Head: <sha> on line 2.

RESOURCE RULES (the bench sandbox caps a job at 256 processes and 8 GiB). Every package-wide go command and make target runs under nice with -p 4 (the GOFLAGS export carries -p=4), one at a time: never two at once, never in the background. A test red at head is rerun at the unchanged BASE only for its own package, never the whole tree at BASE. Commit the work before running the internal/ci gate. Write no scratch test files. Never on amd-2 or amd-3: if JOB.md stages the card on either, stop at once with not-done naming the bench, before any build.

RULES.
Work only in the job directory this card names.
Never force-push or rebase a shared branch.
Never kill a process you did not start.
Never start a server on this machine.
No `rm -rf` outside the job directory.
Report what was not done.
No gh, no push: JOB.md's finish does both, from outside the wall. Never merge. Every `go test` gets `-timeout 600s`; every new test opens with `t.Parallel()`; never `go clean`; never git stash.
Unit tests use no real time, sockets, network or outside services: time is an argument and a test clock starts at 1000.0, the transport is faked, and anything that needs the real thing is a functional test run by a make target. A generated unit compiled and run by the package's own runGenerated helper in a t.TempDir() is the house pattern and stays inside the job.
Make the smallest change that does the task, with no refactor beyond what THE TASK names.
Never touch the space game's baseline or delta code. Never run nova-sprint friend stage; a checkout is cloned on the bench from GitHub, never on the studio Mac.
Never read outside the job's working directory; never search /, ~ or /Volumes; read the files this card names and search inside the checkout only.
Schema-table bounds: a bound is declared only if every writer and reader path enforces it; otherwise the field stays open.
Files a change must touch to keep the tree green are always inside PATHS, whatever the brief names: every *_test.go, every file under a testdata/ directory, tla/RUNS.tsv, tla/CASES.tsv and tla/COVERAGE.tsv, internal/docs/catalog.go, and every AGENTS.md map; any other file outside PATHS is still out of scope. ROADMAP.md, docs/roadmap.sexp and docs/roadmap-evidence/*.sexp are lander-owned ledgers inside every card's PATHS: commit the ROADMAP.md that make roadmap writes, never a hand edit.
Touch only the files PATHS names; a fix that needs another file goes into the report as a proposed diff, not a commit. No other language's emitter, runtime or driver is edited, and no other-language card is proposed: that work is parked by the owner. \"Not done\" is a welcome report; a green claim you did not run is not.")
