# go port: bounded memory and time on hostile inputs (go port of schema)

Denial-of-service hardening for the go port (Glenn, 2026-10-18). Using the bounds documented by schema-shared-resource-bounds-every-port (read it first), assert in this port that hostile inputs cost bounded memory and time: huge counts, deep nesting, recursion, giant strings and many unknown fields. Allocation is bounded by input size times the documented factor; nesting depth is capped and refused by name. Use a test with an allocation counter in this language (an allocator hook, a counting allocator, runtime memstats, GC counters or the nearest the language offers; name it). Time is asserted by operation count or step count, never by wall clock. A violation is a finding: red first, then fix in the generator or runtime; fix the port, and carry any cross-port gap as FIX-CARD: <slug>: <one line>.

Files you may touch: the PATHS above, plus the NEW files the card needs (the card's own make file make/schema-go-resource-bounds.mk (the Makefile includes make/*.mk, so no Makefile edit is needed), its own workflow .github/workflows/schema-go-resource-bounds.yml (a schedule and workflow_dispatch trigger for nightly parts, push or pull_request for fast parts), and test files; cards share no workflow or Makefile edits so they land independently. When the work needs a file outside PATHS (the port's test tree, generator, fixtures, saved corpus), report PATHS-PROPOSED with the paths and stop that part as not-done). Do not add needs on other cards: this lens stands alone. Worktree: the staged checkout JOB.md names; branch: the card's own; base: sprint/schema-go.
Libraries considered: the Go standard library and the repository's own tools (tools/negativecontrols, tools/slowgatescan, internal/ci) for gates and tests; the checker or fuzzer named above for the analysis itself, installed fleet-wide from its official source; no hand-written reimplementation of any checker.

DONE-WHEN: the Makefile target exists and passes on the corpus at the card's head; every real finding is fixed with a test that was red at BASE (the failing lines kept); every suppression has a justification; the planted-bad-pattern test is red when the plant is in and green when it is out; CI stays inside its one-minute budget (heavy parts are nightly); the report lists the triage table (finding, REAL or NOT, action) and the other ports' generators that share a flagged pattern.

STEP 1. Enter your worktree with cd <worktree path> && git log --oneline -1; it is a NEW worktree on the branch this card names. Export GOFLAGS=-mod=readonly NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1 before any go command; GOCACHE is already set to the machine's shared build cache (JOB.md names it): keep it. Build and test only on the Linux bench JOB.md names, never the working Mac, and never run the heavy tool on the studio.
STEP 1. COUNTER. Build the allocation counter harness first and prove it counts by a planted unbounded allocation.
STEP 2. CASES. One test per hostile shape (huge count, deep nesting, recursion, giant string, many unknown fields), each at the documented factor and depth cap, each failing at BASE if the port is unbounded there (keep the failing lines).
