# A test fails when Go has a refusal name the spec lacks, or the spec has one Go lacks

OWNER RULE (Glenn, 2026-10-15): \"When you improve schema, improve the standard, and then fix it in golang and verify the fix.\" No other language's emitter is edited.
Source: the comprehensive spec-vs-Go review of 2026-10-18 (Glenn: \"do a *comprehensive* spec review vs. the golang implementation and make sure they match\"; and \"if any change made in golang has not been folded back into the spec, do that NOW\": the language ports are unheld and read the spec). Every row below was found by a cold reader at sprint/schema-go d808c3f54, with the Go file and line it read.

THE TASK. A pure unit test, no build of generated code needed: TestSpecRefusalNamesMatchGo in internal/codegen/gotable/specrefusalnames_test.go, opening with t.Parallel(). It reads docs/SPEC-TABLES.md (path from the package dir, as compiler/docs_test.go (read) finds it), takes the backticked snake_case names in the first column between the refusal-names markers, and takes Go's set from the emitted runtime source (tableRefusalSource in refusal.go: every `case TableRefuseX:return \"name\"`, minus ok) plus every Reason string literal assigned in non-test gotable source (go/ast over the package files and the emitted-source string constants, the \": <Table>.<field>\" suffix cut). It fails naming each name that is in Go but not in the spec, and each that is in the spec but not in Go, and fails if any Reason literal is prose (contains a space). It includes a reversed witness: a sub-test that runs the comparison on a copy of the spec text with one name removed, and on a Go set with one name added, and asserts each is reported.

DONE-WHEN: TestSpecRefusalNamesMatchGo RUNs and PASSes at head (red at BASE: no tests to run); its reversed witnesses report the planted missing name in each direction; the gate passes or its only reds are named as red at BASE.

