# testgen emits a fuzz target per table: no panic, and every flip refused by name or canonical

BOUNDED SEED CORPUS (Rowan, 2026-10-18; the harness stays, this bounds only what it seeds). Pass 5 found the unbounded corpus unbuildable: one WideBlob sample of about 280k bytes, with a 0, 1 and 0xff mutation at every byte, reached 94 GiB RSS and millions of f.Add cases. So, per table (per Fuzz<Table>Wire, Text and Json target):
- Emit at most 256 mutation seeds, chosen deterministically: the first 16 and last 16 byte positions, then a fixed stride over the positions between them, each position taking all three values 0, 1 and 0xff, stopping at the cap. Where the stride set is smaller than the cap, all of it is emitted. The unmutated sample encoding is always also seeded.
- No sample used for seeding exceeds 4 KiB. A larger sample is trimmed to a representative 4 KiB (the first 2 KiB and the last 2 KiB) before it is seeded or mutated, and the generated code carries a comment saying so. Trimming is for seeding only; the round-trip tests keep the full sample.
- A test pins the seed count (at most 256 per target) and the total seed bytes across the generated file, so the corpus cannot silently grow again; the test fails when either exceeds its pinned bound.
- Peak RSS of `go test` of the generated fuzz package stays under 1 GiB. DONE-WHEN measures it as a gate: run under the bench's `/usr/bin/time -v` (Maximum resident set size) and state the figure in the report; if the test can bound it in-process (runtime.ReadMemStats after the seeds run), it does so too.
- The fuzzer still explores beyond the seeds at fuzz time; the cap bounds the seed corpus only. The reader checks and the three assertions per input are unchanged.
This amends THE TASK's \"every single-byte flip of them\" and DONE-WHEN's \"every flip of every seed\" to: the bounded set above. The docs/SPEC-TABLES.md rule says the same (seeds are bounded and deterministic).

THE ONE THING LEFT (2026-10-18): the harness is built and is not to be rebuilt. It is at head 76facf0f3e7fa44f87563c7e0cdb2e1709b4c9ca on branch sprint/schema-go-testgen-fuzz-target-per-table.w2.g1.e21 (the w1 work, head 0e6789189, with the BASE tip merged). Its DONE-WHEN gate was red only on three reader defects, now their own cards: FuzzPatrolWire/bool_payload_not_0_or_1 (schema-go-wire-bool-not-0-or-1), FuzzWeaponConfigText/base64_padding_bits_not_zero (schema-go-text-base64-canonical) and FuzzWideBlobJson/json_string_ill_formed_utf8 (schema-go-json-refuses-invalid-utf8). This card waits on all three. Once they land: start from that head, merge the BASE tip, rerun DONE-WHEN and the STEP 5 and 5b gates, and report; the three inputs now pass. Fix no reader here; a new defect the corpus finds is reported, not fixed. The input-normalised comparison is the follow-on schema-go-fuzz-compare-normalised-input, not this card.

OWNER RULE (Glenn, 2026-10-15 12:34Z): \"When you improve schema, improve the standard, and then fix it in golang and verify the fix. Stop the multi-language stuff until we have finished our dogfooding in golang.\" The rule is written in docs/SPEC-TABLES.md first, in a commit of its own, and Go is then made to match it; no other language's emitter is edited, and every other target refuses a new construct by name, centrally, in compiler/generate.go, as compiler/tablesstringarrays.go does for text lists. Worked example: the landed card schema-string-arrays-and-top-level-array-roots (standard commit, then Go, central refusal, red-then-green tests).

SCOPE (Glenn, 2026-10-17): card all the schema Go improvements so schema Go reaches 100% before any port; this card is one of them.

Source. Glenn's list 2026-10-17 item 16: a flipped byte panicked decide's ParseSchema (a nil map write); generated readers must never panic on damaged input.

RED AT BASE (sprint/schema-go @ caea41783528, hetzner1, 2026-10-17):
- `schema testgen x` prints the usage and exits 2 at sprint/schema-go @ caea41783528 (no testgen, so no generated fuzz target exists); the repo's Fuzz functions are FuzzReadText, FuzzFormatIdempotent, FuzzPipeline, FuzzPipelineTwoFiles and FuzzGeneratedCompiles.
- `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/gotable/ -run '^TestGoTestgenFuzzTargets$' -v` prints `testing: warning: no tests to run` and `ok ... [no tests to run]` (no RUN line; the test is new)

THE TASK. (1) Standard: the generated-test section says testgen emits, per root table, Fuzz<Table>Wire, Fuzz<Table>Text and Fuzz<Table>Json, each seeded with the sample value's own encodings and with every single-byte flip of them, asserting for every input: the read never panics; it is refused with a named reason, or it decodes to a value whose re-encoding is exactly the input (so a silent wrong decode can only be a canonical one, which the integrity check, when declared, refuses). (2) Go: testgen emits them in the generated test file. A panic or a non-canonical accept found while testing the corpus is a defect in the reader: name it in the report with the input, commit the input under the generated package's testdata/fuzz only in the t.TempDir() run, and do not fix it here.

DONE-WHEN: `SCHEMA_SLOW=1 go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/gotable/ -run '^TestGoTestgenFuzzTargets$' -v` RUN and PASS: testgen of tables/examples in a t.TempDir() emits the three targets per root table, `go test` runs each bounded seed corpus and passes, the seed-count and seed-bytes pin test passes, peak RSS of that go test is under 1 GiB (measured, figure reported), and a reader with one bounds check removed by the test makes a target fail. The report lists every defect found. The STEP 5 gate is green or its only reds are named as present at BASE; STEP 5b shows zero differences.

STEP 1. cd into the staged checkout JOB.md names and run git log --oneline -1; it is a NEW worktree on the branch this card names. Record BASE_SHA=$(git rev-parse HEAD) for STEP 5b. Export GOFLAGS='-mod=readonly -p=4' NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1 before any go command; GOCACHE is already set to the machine's shared build cache (JOB.md names it): keep it. Build and test only on the Linux bench JOB.md names, never the working Mac.
STEP 2. The standard first: write the rule into docs/SPEC-TABLES.md (the sections THE TASK names; SPEC.md too where it says so) and the short docs/USAGE.md entry, and commit that alone, before any code.
STEP 3. Red first: write the tests TEST and THE TASK name, each opening with t.Parallel(); run each and keep its failing line.
STEP 4. Make them pass in the files PATHS names, citing the section of the standard from each function that implements a rule.
STEP 5. Run the gate: go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/gotable/ ./compiler/ ./internal/check/ ./internal/parser/ ./ir/ ./internal/ci/ and read the last line of each; then make test-go, and make conformance SCHEMA_SKIP_LEGS=cs,java,elixir,dart,js and read the go column (a red in another leg's column is named and compared against BASE, never fixed here), all on the bench. When a test fails, name its file and say whether that file was changed by your work (yours) or is unchanged (already red at BASE: run the same test on the unchanged base to say so), and report that line first.
STEP 5b. GENERATED OUTPUT, base against head. Build two compilers inside the job: mkdir -p .cmp/base; git archive $BASE_SHA | tar -x -C .cmp/base; go build -C .cmp/base -o $PWD/.cmp/schema-base ./cmd/schema; go build -o .cmp/schema-head ./cmd/schema; add .cmp to .git/info/exclude so it is never committed. Then for every file f in git ls-files of *.schema at BASE, and for each of --lang go and --lang cpp, run both binaries as generate --lang L --out .cmp/out/{base,head}/<f with / as _> f, keeping each exit status and stderr; compare them, then diff -rq the two output trees. Report the count of files compared and every difference. INTENDED DIFFERENCES: none. Every difference is a defect of this card: zero differences is required.
STEP 6. Commit on your own branch with the trailer. Nothing reaches the forge from inside the wall: in the job the git shim records a push, the pull request is the finish JOB.md names (the last STEP), and the member makes both, against the BASE: line's branch, from outside the wall when the card finishes. The pull request body states the diff stat, the tests with what each pins, the STEP 5b count and differences, and what was not done.
STEP 7. End as JOB.md says: where JOB.md ends the card with its pull request, that is the end and there is nothing else to write, the gate's lines in the pull request body; where it asks for RESULT.md, write it in JOB.md's shape (head, branch, verdict, gate, output, report). The report starts with Verdict: LAND or Verdict: HOLD on line 1 and Head: <sha> on line 2.

