# The Java emitter writes the null-sentinel pattern without nullness annotations: NullAway reports 19,789 findings on the generated corpus

RULING (coordinator, 2026-10-19, on j29257): the NullAway corpus gate (schema-java-generated-errorprone-nullaway-gate) is red on 19,789 findings, all from the null-sentinel pattern in generated Java. We do NOT blanket-suppress them. The javatable emitter emits nullness annotations instead. The gate card waits on this one: it cannot pass without the annotations.

THE TASK. (1) Annotation set: JSpecify. Check first which set the gate's Error Prone and NullAway configuration expects (read the gate branch above): NullAway honours any annotation named Nullable and, from 0.12, the JSpecify annotations with its JSpecifyMode option; if the gate's config names a different set (javax.annotation.Nullable, androidx, a custom one), report it at once as HOLD with the file and line and do not guess; the default is org.jspecify.annotations.Nullable and @NullMarked. (2) In internal/codegen/javatable, make the emitter write `@NullMarked` on the generated package or each generated top-level type (one place, by the JSpecify rules: package-info.java if the output layout allows one, else the type) and `@Nullable` on every field, parameter, return and type argument that can hold the null sentinel, and on nothing else; a field the emitter initialises to a non-null value in every constructor is not annotated. Derive the sentinel sites from the emitter's own code: the report lists each emitter function that now writes an annotation and the generated shape it annotates. The generated runtime, reader and writer classes are all covered, not only the tables. (3) The annotations must not change behaviour or the wire: generated bytes of every class are identical except the added imports and annotations; say in the report how you checked that (a diff of the generated tree at BASE and at head with annotation lines and imports filtered out must be empty). (4) Keep the generated Java compiling with the Java toolchain the Makefile's java targets use, with the jspecify jar on the classpath where the generated tests and the corpus compile; add the jar the way the other Java dependencies are fetched, and name the pinned version and checksum in the report. (5) Regenerate the Java trees (find the make targets with grep inside the checkout) and commit what they write; after merging the base tip, rerun the IsCurrent-style tests on the merged tree.

THE TESTS, each opening with t.Parallel():
- TestJavaEmitterEmitsNullnessAnnotations, internal/codegen/javatable/nullness_test.go: pure Go over the generated Java text of the corpus units: the NullMarked marker is present on every generated type or package, every sentinel site (the table the emitter functions of item 2 define, in the test as data) carries @Nullable, and no non-sentinel field does. Red at BASE.
- The NullAway proof, a functional test run by a make target and skipped by name when the JDK or the jars are absent and SCHEMA_REQUIRE_CORPUS is unset (failing by name when it is 1): javac with Error Prone and NullAway (the flags the gate's make file uses, JSpecify mode, warnings as errors) over a generated unit that holds the sentinel pattern reports ZERO NullAway findings at head and the 19,789-style findings (print the count) at BASE. Red at BASE.
- Reversed witness: in a throwaway copy of the generated unit inside the job drop one @Nullable from one sentinel field or return, run the same javac line, and show it reports at least one NullAway finding naming that site; record the line. Add it as a Go test that plants the same drop in the emitter's output text and asserts TestJavaEmitterEmitsNullnessAnnotations goes red.

OVERLAP. This card touches the emitter path that many Java cards share, so it lands AFTER the open Java cards that edit javatable, never before them (land-after, not needs); the cards whose PATHS overlap are named on the admit and listed in the report. Do not rebase onto them: merge the base tip and regenerate.

DONE-WHEN: `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/javatable/ -run '^TestJavaEmitterEmitsNullnessAnnotations$' -v` RUNs and PASSes at head (red at BASE); the NullAway functional make target reports zero findings on a generated unit at head and its BASE count is in the report; the reversed witness goes red; `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/javatable/ ./compiler/ ./internal/ci/` is green or its only reds are named as present at BASE; the generated-tree diff with annotations filtered is empty.
STEP 1. cd into the staged checkout JOB.md names and run git log --oneline -1; it is a NEW worktree on the branch this card names. Record BASE_SHA=$(git rev-parse HEAD). Export GOFLAGS='-mod=readonly -p=4' NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1 before any go command; GOCACHE is already set to the machine's shared build cache (JOB.md names it): keep it. Build and test only on the Linux bench JOB.md names, never the working Mac.
STEP 2. Red first: write the tests TEST and THE TASK name, each opening with t.Parallel(); run each and keep its failing line.
STEP 3. Make them pass in the files PATHS names, citing the section of the standard from each function that implements a rule.
STEP 4. Run the gate: the go test lines of DONE-WHEN, then go test -count=1 -timeout 600s on the other packages DONE-WHEN names, and read the last line of each. When a test fails, name its file and say whether that file was changed by your work (yours) or is unchanged (already red at BASE: run the same test on the unchanged base to say so), and report that line first.
STEP 5. Commit on your own branch with the trailer. Nothing reaches the forge from inside the wall: in the job the git shim records a push, the pull request is the finish JOB.md names (the last STEP), and the member makes both, against the BASE: line's branch, from outside the wall when the card finishes. The pull request body states the diff stat, the tests with what each pins, and what was not done.
STEP 6. End as JOB.md says: where JOB.md ends the card with its pull request, that is the end and there is nothing else to write, the gate's lines in the pull request body; where it asks for RESULT.md, write it in JOB.md's shape (head, branch, verdict, gate, output, report). The report starts with Verdict: LAND or Verdict: HOLD on line 1 and Head: <sha> on line 2.
RESOURCE RULES (the bench sandbox caps a job at 256 processes and 8 GiB). Every package-wide go command and make target runs under nice with -p 4, one at a time: never two at once, never in the background. Never on amd-2 or amd-3: if JOB.md stages the card on either, stop at once with not-done naming the bench, before any build.
