# checkJs strict: codec and union literals do not match the shared descriptor type (~1,760 findings)

Source. The coordinator, 2026-10-19. Card schema-js-emitter-jsdoc-types (branch sprint/schema-js-emitter-jsdoc-types.w1.g1.e21, head 2b130c5e3) brought `tsc --checkJs --strict` over the generated JS corpus from 41,066 findings to 7,642 with JSDoc only; its report (NOT DONE 1) names the groups that remain, all needing real code changes. Card name of the whole: schema-js-checkjs-strict-to-zero, split into one card per group plus a last card that sets the gate's ratchet to 0. This card is one group. Real code changes in the JS emitter and the runtimes it emits are allowed. THE HARD RULE: the wire bytes stay byte-identical.

THE TASK. The emitted codec and union literals do not match the linked TableVariableCodec typedef, and the class-versus-Rec boundary is typed inconsistently (about 1,760 of the 7,642). Make the typedef describe the literals the emitter writes (a discriminated union per shape where the literals differ, with the discriminant the code already carries), and make each literal satisfy it; type the class / Rec boundary once, where a generated class is handed to code that expects a Rec. The literal VALUES and their order do not change, so the encoded bytes do not. Bring this group's findings to zero.

THE TESTS, each opening with t.Parallel(), no real time: TestJsCodecLiteralsMatchTheDescriptorType in internal/codegen/jstable/checkjscodecliterals_test.go, pure Go over the in-process generated JavaScript text of the corpus units (as jsdoctypes_test.go does): every emitted codec and union literal names a member of the descriptor typedef and carries every property that member requires, and the typedef has no `any`, `*` or `Object` member. REVERSED WITNESS in the same file: undo the fix in a t.TempDir() copy of one generated unit (a planted text edit of the emitted output) and show the check goes red naming the function; record the tsc line the plant brings back.

THE WIRE PROOF (required, in the report): (1) the cross-port conformance corpus: run `make conformance` with SCHEMA_SKIP_LEGS naming every leg except go and js (read the leg list in the Makefile; grep -n conformance Makefile make/*.mk) at BASE_SHA and at head, both green on the js column; (2) a byte diff of encoded outputs: for every fixture the js conformance leg (and the js bench drivers) ENCODE, keep the encoded bytes at BASE_SHA and at head in two directories inside the job and `cmp`/`diff -r` them; the count of files compared and ZERO differences go in the report. A difference in any encoded byte is a defect of this card, never a test to update.

DONE-WHEN: `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/jstable/ -run '^TestJsCodecLiteralsMatchTheDescriptorType$' -v` RUNs and PASSes at head (red at BASE); THE COUNT: after each emitter change, regenerate (the repository's make targets for the JS trees and build/tables-generated-js, as make/js.mk names them) and rerun tsc over the corpus the checkjs gate reads (generated/js, generated/js-ludicrous, generated/bench/js, generated/bench/paired/js, build/tables-generated-js) with `--checkJs --strict --noEmit --allowJs` and TypeScript 5.9.3; keep the count by TS code at BASE_SHA and at head in the report. The findings of this group (TableVariableCodec / union literal assignability and the class vs Rec boundary, mostly TS2322, TS2345, TS2353, TS2741) are ZERO at head. THE WIRE PROOF shows zero differences; `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/jstable/ ./internal/codegen/js/ ./internal/goldens/ ./internal/ci/` is green or its only reds are named as present at BASE (schema-js-emitter-jsdoc-types reported 5 compiler reds at BASE 8ea510762: TestMapCarriers, TestMapRefusalNamesTheCarrier, TestOptionalArraysCarriers, TestUnionArraysCarriers, TestWasRowsCarriers).

STEP 1. cd into the staged checkout JOB.md names and run git log --oneline -1; it is a NEW worktree on the branch this card names. Record BASE_SHA=$(git rev-parse HEAD). Export GOFLAGS='-mod=readonly -p=4' NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1 before any go command; GOCACHE is already set to the machine's shared build cache (JOB.md names it): keep it. Build and test only on the Linux bench JOB.md names, never the working Mac.
STEP 2. Red first: write the test TEST names, opening with t.Parallel(); run it and keep its failing line; record the BASE tsc count for this card's group.
STEP 3. Make it pass in the files PATHS names, citing the docs/SPEC-TABLES.md section from each emitter function whose emitted code you change. Regenerate every tree the change moves with its make target and commit what it writes, never a hand edit of generated output.
STEP 4. Run the gate: the go test lines of DONE-WHEN, then go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./internal/codegen/jstable/ ./internal/codegen/js/ ./internal/goldens/ ./internal/ci/, and THE WIRE PROOF. When a test fails, name its file and say whether that file was changed by your work (yours) or is unchanged (already red at BASE: run the same test on the unchanged base to say so), and report that line first.
STEP 5. Commit on your own branch with the trailer. Nothing reaches the forge from inside the wall: in the job the git shim records a push, the pull request is the finish JOB.md names (the last STEP), and the member makes both, against the BASE: line's branch, from outside the wall when the card finishes. The pull request body states the diff stat, the tests with what each pins, the tsc counts by code at BASE and head, the wire proof, and what was not done.
STEP 6. End as JOB.md says: where JOB.md ends the card with its pull request, that is the end and there is nothing else to write, the gate's lines in the pull request body; where it asks for RESULT.md, write it in JOB.md's shape (head, branch, verdict, gate, output, report). The report starts with Verdict: LAND or Verdict: HOLD on line 1 and Head: <sha> on line 2.

