Source. The coordinator, 2026-10-19. Card schema-js-emitter-jsdoc-types (branch sprint/schema-js-emitter-jsdoc-types.w1.g1.e21, head 2b130c5e3) brought `tsc --checkJs --strict` over the generated JS corpus from 41,066 findings to 7,642 with JSDoc only; its report (NOT DONE 1) names the groups that remain, all needing real code changes. Card name of the whole: schema-js-checkjs-strict-to-zero, split into one card per group plus a last card that sets the gate's ratchet to 0. This card is one group. Real code changes in the JS emitter and the runtimes it emits are allowed. THE HARD RULE: the wire bytes stay byte-identical.

THE TASK. Once every group card has landed, `tsc --checkJs --strict` over the generated JS corpus reports zero findings. Set the checkjs gate's ratchet to zero: the per-code baseline SCHEMA_JS_CHECKJS_BASELINE in make/schema-js-generated-checkjs-strict-eslint-gate.mk (today a list of `code:count` pairs) allows no finding of any code, and the gate goes red on the first finding. internal/ci/schema_js_checkjs_ratchet.mjs's parseBaseline refuses an empty baseline today: give it one explicit spelling for \"no finding allowed\" (for example `none`), with a --self-test case for it, rather than an empty string, so a missing value still fails loudly. Update the gate's Go test (internal/ci/schema_js_generated_checkjs_strict_eslint_gate_test.go) to pin the zero baseline. The planted-pattern negative control (make/negative-controls.json, plant file generated/js/Degenerate.js (read)) stays and still turns the gate red. If any finding remains at the BASE tip when you start, do not raise the baseline: report it by code and file and HOLD.

THE TESTS, each opening with t.Parallel(), no real time: TestSchemaJsCheckjsRatchetIsZero in internal/ci/schema_js_checkjs_ratchet_zero_test.go: pure Go, reads the make file and asserts the baseline is the zero spelling (red at BASE: the baseline is a non-zero list). The ratchet's --self-test (node, run by the gate's make target) has a case where the zero baseline meets one TS7006 line and fails naming it, and one where it meets a clean log and passes. REVERSED WITNESS: plant one TS7006 in a t.TempDir() copy of a generated file (or run the existing negative control) and show the gate's checkjs target exits non-zero naming the finding.

