# Rust clippy and Miri gate (rust port of schema)

THE LENS. Schema generates the same algorithm in many languages, so every language's strictest checker, run over the generated output for the schema repository's test-schema corpus (every schema under the corpus directories the Makefile's generate targets read, found with grep -n in the Makefile and make/*.mk; do not invent a second corpus), is a separate lens on bugs (Glenn, 2026-10-18; ideas roadmap group schema-per-language-lenses). THIS CARD: clippy -D warnings (pedantic, with justified allows) over the runtime and generated units, and Miri over the runtime and generated units tests (undefined behaviour); also forbid(unsafe_code) where the unit needs no unsafe, and overflow-checks = true in release over the generated rust output (readers and writers, and the runtime where the port has one). Miri is slow: nightly, with the smallest test set that still covers every reader kind.

Files you may touch: the PATHS above, plus the NEW files the card needs (the card's own make file make/schema-rust-generated-clippy-miri-gate.mk (the Makefile includes make/*.mk, but on this BASE the port registry has not landed, so every make/*.mk stem is probed as a --lang port: when `grep -c '^SCHEMA_PORTS :=' Makefile` prints 0, apply the Makefile hunk of the landed port-list commit 6f993bea byte-identical (`git show 6f993bea -- Makefile | git apply`) and make no other Makefile edit), its own workflow .github/workflows/schema-rust-generated-clippy-miri-gate.yml (a schedule and workflow_dispatch trigger for nightly parts, push or pull_request for fast parts), and test files; cards share no workflow edits and no Makefile edit beyond that byte-identical hunk, so they land independently. When the work needs a file outside PATHS (the port's test tree, generator, fixtures, saved corpus), report PATHS-PROPOSED with the paths and stop that part as not-done). Do not add needs on other cards: this lens stands alone. Worktree: the staged checkout JOB.md names; branch: the card's own; base: sprint/schema-rust.
Libraries considered: the Go standard library and the repository's own tools (tools/negativecontrols, tools/slowgatescan, internal/ci) for gates and tests; the checker or fuzzer named above for the analysis itself, installed fleet-wide from its official source; no hand-written reimplementation of any checker.

DONE-WHEN: the Makefile target exists and passes on the corpus at the card's head; every real finding is fixed with a test that was red at BASE (the failing lines kept); every suppression has a justification; the planted-bad-pattern test is red when the plant is in and green when it is out; CI stays inside its one-minute budget (heavy parts are nightly); the report lists the triage table (finding, REAL or NOT, action) and the other ports' generators that share a flagged pattern.

STEP 1. Enter your worktree with cd <worktree path> && git log --oneline -1; it is a NEW worktree on the branch this card names. Export GOFLAGS=-mod=readonly NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1 before any go command; GOCACHE is already set to the machine's shared build cache (JOB.md names it): keep it. Build and test only on the Linux bench JOB.md names, never the working Mac, and never run the heavy tool on the studio.
STEP 1. INSTALL. Install the tool fleet-wide from its official source (Glenn's standing grant: the fleet admin installs software through the fleet ansible play in rowan-tools/fleet, never by hand on one machine, never from an unofficial mirror). If you cannot reach the play from the wall, make the Makefile target probe for the tool and report 'tool missing: <name>' as not-done, naming the machines lacking it; never fake a pass. Pin the tool's version in the Makefile.
STEP 2. TRIAGE. Run the tool once over the generated output for the whole corpus and keep the full finding list in the pull request body. Every finding is either REAL or NOT. A REAL finding is fixed in the generator (never by hand-editing generated files), with a Go or port-native test that is RED at BASE and green after the fix; keep the failing line. A finding that is NOT real is suppressed in the narrowest place the tool allows, with a one-line justification beside the suppression; no blanket disables. A bug one checker finds in one port often exists in the others: grep the other ports' generators for the same pattern and name the hits in the report (do not fix other ports in this card).
STEP 3. GATE. Add a Makefile target (in the card's own make file) and wire it into CI through the card's own workflow file. Fast tools run on push and the whole CI must stay inside its one-minute budget (measure it and report the added seconds). Heavy tools (Frama-C EVA, Kani, CBMC, Miri, MSan, Dialyzer, differentials, fuzzing) go in a scheduled (nightly) trigger of the card's own workflow with a time budget, never per push.
STEP 4. PROOF THE GATE FIRES. Plant a known-bad pattern the tool must catch (in a scratch copy of the generated output or through a generator switch that only a test turns on), add a test that the target exits non-zero and names the plant, and keep the red line. Follow the repo's negative-control convention (make/negative-controls.json, tools/negativecontrols): register the control there.
STEP 6. Commit on your own branch with the trailer. Nothing reaches the forge from inside the wall: in the job the git shim records a push, the pull request is the finish JOB.md names (the last STEP), and the member makes both, against sprint/schema-rust, from outside the wall when the card finishes. The pull request body states the diff stat, the tests with what each pins, and what was not done.
STEP 7. End as JOB.md says (docs/SPEC-CARD-CONTRACT.md): where JOB.md ends the card with its pull request, that is the end and there is nothing else to write, the gate's lines in the pull request body; where it asks for RESULT.md, write it in JOB.md's shape (head, branch, verdict, gate, output, report). The report starts with Verdict: LAND or Verdict: HOLD on line 1 and Head: <sha> on line 2.

RULES.
Work only in the job directory this card names.
Never force-push or rebase a shared branch.
Never kill a process you did not start.
Never start a server on this machine.
No `rm -rf` outside the job directory.
Report what was not done.
No gh, no push: JOB.md's finish does both, from outside the wall. Never merge. Every `go test` gets `-timeout 600s`; every new test opens with `t.Parallel()`.
Unit tests use no real time, no sockets, no network and no outside services: time is an argument and a test clock starts at 1000.0. Fuzzing, analyzers that run for minutes and anything needing the network are functional tier, behind the slow gate (read internal/slowtest), never in a unit test.
Install tools only from official sources and only through the fleet play; never print or store a secret or token.
Make the smallest change that does the task, with no refactor beyond what the task names.
Never read outside the job's working directory; never search /, ~ or /Volumes; search inside the checkout only.
ROADMAP.md and docs/roadmap.sexp are lander-owned ledgers inside every card's PATHS: commit the ROADMAP.md the roadmap tool renders if the card's work moves an item.
A test that SKIPS is not a pass: a gate line must show --- PASS. A red is pre-existing only if reproduced at BASE in the same environment.
Finish LAND with --head <sha>; finish HOLD with --failed. \"Not done\" is a welcome report; a green claim you did not run is not.")
