# The cross-language test/table-base64 harness expects `Q=Q==` to give kind_mismatch; section 16.2 and the ruling say base64_not_canonical, and the tolerant read accepts it

Source. The coordinator, 2026-10-19. The harness expects an `=` in the middle of a base64 body (`Q=Q==`) to be refused as `kind_mismatch`. docs/SPEC-TABLES.md section 16.2 and the coordinator's ruling on schema-go-json-base64-canonical say it is `base64_not_canonical` (a refusal on the strict read; the tolerant read accepts it and counts it). The harness is also already red at BASE on a different case.

THE TASK. (1) Align EVERY case of the harness with section 16.2, not only `Q=Q==`: go through the case table line by line against the section's text and the Go oracle (the Go reader's answer for the same input on the strict and the tolerant read), and change the expected answer of each case that disagrees; list each changed case (input, old expectation, new) in the report. Where section 16.2 is silent or ambiguous about a case, do not guess: leave it out of the table, name it, and say what the Go oracle answers. If the standard itself needs a sentence, write it in a commit of its own first. (2) THE BASE RED: first run the harness at BASE and keep the failing case line. Find its cause. If the harness expectation is wrong, fix it under item 1. If it is a REAL defect in a port or in the Go reader, do not fix a port here: list it as REAL with the input, the port and a proposed card (id, stream, one line); a REAL defect in the Go reader of this base is fixed here in the smallest way with a test, and named. (3) Run the harness against EVERY port that carries base64 text and is runnable on the bench (Go, C, C++, C#, Java, JS, Dart, Elixir, Lua, Luau, Squirrel, Rust: use the harness's own driver list, and say which ports the harness does not reach): print a table of port, passes, fails, failing case. Each port that fails becomes a PROPOSED port card in the report (id, stream, base, one line) unless the port is already correct and the harness is wrong; a port not yet carrying base64 (its carrier card is still waiting) is listed as not-carried, not failed. Do not edit any port. (4) Reversed witness: flip the `Q=Q==` expectation back to kind_mismatch in a throwaway copy inside the job and show the harness goes red naming that case; add it as a test that asserts the harness's expected answer for `Q=Q==` is base64_not_canonical.

THE TESTS, each opening with t.Parallel(), no real time:
- TestBase64HarnessFollowsSpec162, test/table-base64/harness_spec162_test.go: reads the harness's case table and asserts, for each case section 16.2 states, the expected answer is the section's (a table in the test with the section's sentence cited per row), and that `Q=Q==` expects base64_not_canonical on the strict read and an accepted, counted value on the tolerant read. Reversed witness in the same test as item 4. Check every helper's returned error (internal/ci TestGeneratedHelperResultsAreChecked).

DONE-WHEN: `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./test/table-base64/ -run '^TestBase64HarnessFollowsSpec162$' -v` RUNs and PASSes at head (red at BASE); the harness itself runs green for the Go driver at head and the base red is explained; the per-port table is in the report with the proposed port cards; `go test -count=1 -timeout 600s ./test/... ./internal/ci/` is green or its only reds are named as present at BASE.
STEP 1. cd into the staged checkout JOB.md names and run git log --oneline -1; it is a NEW worktree on the branch this card names. Record BASE_SHA=$(git rev-parse HEAD). Export GOFLAGS='-mod=readonly -p=4' NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1 before any go command; GOCACHE is already set to the machine's shared build cache (JOB.md names it): keep it. Build and test only on the Linux bench JOB.md names, never the working Mac.
STEP 2. Red first: write the tests TEST and THE TASK name, each opening with t.Parallel(); run each and keep its failing line.
STEP 3. Make them pass in the files PATHS names, citing the section of the standard from each function that implements a rule.
STEP 4. Run the gate: the go test lines of DONE-WHEN, then go test -count=1 -timeout 600s on the other packages DONE-WHEN names, and read the last line of each. When a test fails, name its file and say whether that file was changed by your work (yours) or is unchanged (already red at BASE: run the same test on the unchanged base to say so), and report that line first.
STEP 5. Commit on your own branch with the trailer. Nothing reaches the forge from inside the wall: in the job the git shim records a push, the pull request is the finish JOB.md names (the last STEP), and the member makes both, against the BASE: line's branch, from outside the wall when the card finishes. The pull request body states the diff stat, the tests with what each pins, and what was not done.
STEP 6. End as JOB.md says: where JOB.md ends the card with its pull request, that is the end and there is nothing else to write, the gate's lines in the pull request body; where it asks for RESULT.md, write it in JOB.md's shape (head, branch, verdict, gate, output, report). The report starts with Verdict: LAND or Verdict: HOLD on line 1 and Head: <sha> on line 2.
RESOURCE RULES (the bench sandbox caps a job at 256 processes and 8 GiB). Every package-wide go command and make target runs under nice with -p 4, one at a time: never two at once, never in the background. Never on amd-2 or amd-3: if JOB.md stages the card on either, stop at once with not-done naming the bench, before any build.
RULES.
Work only in the job directory this card names.
Never force-push or rebase a shared branch.
Never kill a process you did not start.
Never start a server on this machine.
No `rm -rf` outside the job directory.
Report what was not done.
No gh, no push: JOB.md's finish does both, from outside the wall. Never merge. Every `go test` gets `-timeout 600s`; every new test opens with `t.Parallel()`; never `go clean`; never git stash.
Unit tests use no real time, sockets, network or outside services: time is an argument and a test clock starts at 1000.0, the transport is faked, and anything that needs the real thing is a functional test run by a make target. A generated unit compiled and run by the package's own runGenerated helper in a t.TempDir() is the house pattern and stays inside the job.
Make the smallest change that does the task, with no refactor beyond what THE TASK names.
Never touch the space game's baseline or delta code. Never run nova-sprint friend stage; a checkout is cloned on the bench from GitHub, never on the studio Mac.
Never read outside the job's working directory; never search /, ~ or /Volumes; read the files this card names and search inside the checkout only.
Files a change must touch to keep the tree green are always inside PATHS, whatever the brief names: every *_test.go, every file under a testdata/ directory, internal/docs/catalog.go, and every AGENTS.md map; any other file outside PATHS is still out of scope. ROADMAP.md, docs/roadmap.sexp and docs/roadmap-evidence/*.sexp are lander-owned ledgers inside every card's PATHS: commit the ROADMAP.md that make roadmap writes, never a hand edit.
Touch only the files PATHS names; a fix that needs another file goes into the report as a proposed diff, not a commit. No other language's emitter, runtime or driver is edited, and no other-language card is proposed: that work is parked by the owner. \"Not done\" is a welcome report; a green claim you did not run is not.")
