# schema CI: certify.yml (nightly) on the fleet

THE RULE (owner, 2026-10-18): \"all CI should run on our fleet, not github.\" Every job in every workflow runs on the fleet's self-hosted runners, never on a GitHub-hosted label (ubuntu-*, macos-*, windows-*), and never through a fallback expression that resolves to one. A job the fleet cannot run today is never sent to a hosted runner: it is DISABLED explicitly (`if: false` plus a comment `# DISABLED: <reason>; owed: <what turns it back on>`) and its workflow+job pair is a named entry in the lint's exception list with the same reason. Windows jobs are disabled until the Windows fleet machine arrives (no Windows runner is registered; the captain runners are WSL2 Linux and are labelled `slow`). Second rule (owner, 2026-10-18): a workflow never installs tools onto a fleet runner (`npm install -g/--global`, `go install`, `cargo install`, `pip install` outside a job-local venv, `dotnet tool install -g`, `sudo apt-get install`, `brew install`, `luarocks install` without a job-local --tree): the runners are shared machines and a global install overwrites the fleet's pinned toolchain under ~/sdk. The fleet installs and pins every tool through its ansible play; a workflow instead runs a short presence check that the pinned tool is on PATH at the pinned version (for example `tsc --version` prints 5.9.3, `lua -v` prints 5.4.8, `staticcheck -version` prints 2026.2.2, `command -v nilaway`) and fails with `TOOL-MISSING <name> <want> <got>` naming the runner (`$RUNNER_NAME`) when it is absent or different. Never fake a pass. `actions/setup-*` steps already guarded by `if: runner.environment == 'github-hosted'` never run on the fleet and may stay; an unguarded `setup-*` step on a job that moves to the fleet is replaced by the presence check. THE BUDGET (owner, 2026-10-16): the per-push/per-PR CI is one minute on average and two at most, end to end; slow parts are nightly or dispatched and gate nothing. Moving a job must not slow the per-PR wall time: measure it on the fleet and report the seconds before and after; keep `concurrency` groups with `cancel-in-progress: true` for pull requests.

THE TASK. THIS REPOSITORY. mas-bandwidth/schema is PUBLIC. Its fleet runners are a repository set registered on this repo: labels `[self-hosted, Linux, X64, fleet, schema]` (four Linux X64 runners) and `[self-hosted, Linux, X64, slow, schema]` (two weak machines, never for the one-minute tier). There is NO macOS and NO Windows runner in the schema set. Because the repository is public, a pull request from a fork must never run on fleet hardware: every fleet job keeps (or gains) the fork guard `if: github.event_name != 'pull_request' || github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository`; a fork's pull request therefore gets no CI run, and a maintainer runs it by workflow_dispatch after reading the diff (say this in a comment once at the top of each workflow you touch; never route forks to a hosted runner). `pull_request_target` workflows (cla.yml) run the BASE branch's code and never check out the pull request's head; they may run on the fleet as long as no step checks out or executes the head. The toolchains (go, jdk 21, elixir/OTP, dart, node with TypeScript 5.9.3, dotnet, rust, lua 5.4.8, luau, staticcheck 2026.2.2, nilaway, gcc/g++/clang, make, cmake) are on the runner's PATH from ~/sdk and the bench OS packages. internal/ci (read ci_test.go: `workflows(t, root)` reads every workflow, keyed by base name) holds the repository's workflow tests; .github/actionlint.yaml (read) lists the self-hosted labels actionlint accepts and still names only the retired `studio` and `space` labels. Go: export GOFLAGS=-mod=readonly NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1; every `go test` gets `-timeout 600s`; every new test opens with `t.Parallel()`. Build and test only on the Linux bench JOB.md names, never the working Mac.

certify.yml (schedule and workflow_dispatch only; it gates nothing) has seven jobs on hosted runners: test and inline-gate (matrix os ubuntu-latest + macos-latest, inline-gate's include rows ubuntu go and macos go/cpp/c/rust/cs), release-targets, release-gates, vuln, negative-controls-nightly-matrix and negative-controls-nightly (ubuntu-latest), with about 33 setup-* steps. Move every Linux row and job to `[self-hosted, Linux, X64, fleet, schema]` (a nightly may use the `slow` set instead if its run time would hold the one-minute runners for long; say which), replace unguarded setup-* steps with presence checks, and DISABLE the macOS rows: there is no darwin runner in the schema set, so the macOS rows move out of the matrix into one job per former row with `if: false` and a DISABLED row each (owed: a darwin runner registered for this repository), or, if a matrix row cannot be disabled alone, one disabled macOS job holding those legs. Remove certify.yml's TEMPORARY rows from the exceptions table in internal/ci/fleet_runners_test.go.

DONE-WHEN: (1) red at BASE: `go test -timeout 600s -run 'Fleet' ./internal/ci/` with certify.yml's TEMPORARY rows deleted fails naming every certify job (keep the lines); (2) at head it passes with no certify TEMPORARY row and DISABLED rows only for the macOS legs; (3) `go test -timeout 600s ./internal/ci/` passes whole; (4) a workflow_dispatch of certify is not required, but the Linux jobs' commands are run once on the bench or their absence of a fleet tool is reported as TOOL-MISSING lines.

STEP 1. cd into the staged checkout JOB.md names; git log --oneline -1. Export GOFLAGS=-mod=readonly NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1.
STEP 2. List every job in the workflows this card owns with its current runs-on (expanded through matrix and expressions) and every global-install line; keep that table for the pull request body (workflow, job, was, now).
STEP 3. Make the change. Keep job names and check names unchanged, so branch protection and other tests keep matching.
STEP 4. Run the gate: `go test -timeout 600s ./internal/ci/` (the whole package, since other tests in it pin these workflows) and `go vet ./internal/ci/`; run actionlint over .github/workflows if it is on PATH (report it absent otherwise).
STEP 5. Commit on your own branch as `schema-shared-certify-on-fleet: <what>` with the trailer. The pull request body holds the table, the measured per-PR wall time on the fleet before and after, and the not-done list.
STEP 6. End as JOB.md says (docs/SPEC-CARD-CONTRACT.md).

RULES.
Work only in the job directory this card names.
Never force-push or rebase a shared branch.
Never kill a process you did not start.
Never start a server on this machine.
No `rm -rf` outside the job directory.
Report what was not done.
No gh, no push: JOB.md's finish does both, from outside the wall. Never merge.
Never read outside the job's working directory; never search /, ~ or /Volumes; search inside the checkout only.
Make the smallest change that does the task, with no refactor beyond what the task names.
Unit tests use no real time, no sockets, no network and no outside services: the lint reads the workflow files in the checkout and nothing else.
Never print or store a secret or token; workflow secrets stay as `${{ secrets.NAME }}` references and are never echoed.
Touch only the files PATHS and NEW name; a fix that needs another file goes into the report as PATHS-PROPOSED, not a commit.
A test that SKIPS is not a pass: a gate line must show PASS. A red is pre-existing only if reproduced at BASE in the same environment.
Finish LAND with --head <sha>; finish HOLD with --failed. The report starts with Verdict: LAND or Verdict: HOLD on line 1 and Head: <sha> on line 2. \"Not done\" is a welcome report; a green claim you did not run is not.

ATTRIBUTION: each commit message ends with the trailer line Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>, and a By: line naming the worker who does this attempt above it; a model name is never a By:.")
