# schema CI: the fleet-runner lint, and the small workflows moved to the fleet

THE RULE (owner, 2026-10-18): \"all CI should run on our fleet, not github.\" Every job in every workflow runs on the fleet's self-hosted runners, never on a GitHub-hosted label (ubuntu-*, macos-*, windows-*), and never through a fallback expression that resolves to one. A job the fleet cannot run today is never sent to a hosted runner: it is DISABLED explicitly (`if: false` plus a comment `# DISABLED: <reason>; owed: <what turns it back on>`) and its workflow+job pair is a named entry in the lint's exception list with the same reason. Windows jobs are disabled until the Windows fleet machine arrives (no Windows runner is registered; the captain runners are WSL2 Linux and are labelled `slow`). Second rule (owner, 2026-10-18): a workflow never installs tools onto a fleet runner (`npm install -g/--global`, `go install`, `cargo install`, `pip install` outside a job-local venv, `dotnet tool install -g`, `sudo apt-get install`, `brew install`, `luarocks install` without a job-local --tree): the runners are shared machines and a global install overwrites the fleet's pinned toolchain under ~/sdk. The fleet installs and pins every tool through its ansible play; a workflow instead runs a short presence check that the pinned tool is on PATH at the pinned version (for example `tsc --version` prints 5.9.3, `lua -v` prints 5.4.8, `staticcheck -version` prints 2026.2.2, `command -v nilaway`) and fails with `TOOL-MISSING <name> <want> <got>` naming the runner (`$RUNNER_NAME`) when it is absent or different. Never fake a pass. `actions/setup-*` steps already guarded by `if: runner.environment == 'github-hosted'` never run on the fleet and may stay; an unguarded `setup-*` step on a job that moves to the fleet is replaced by the presence check. THE BUDGET (owner, 2026-10-16): the per-push/per-PR CI is one minute on average and two at most, end to end; slow parts are nightly or dispatched and gate nothing. Moving a job must not slow the per-PR wall time: measure it on the fleet and re