THE TASK. THIS REPOSITORY. mas-bandwidth/schema is PUBLIC. Its fleet runners are a repository set registered on this repo: labels `[self-hosted, Linux, X64, fleet, schema]` (four Linux X64 runners) and `[self-hosted, Linux, X64, slow, schema]` (two weak machines, never for the one-minute tier). There is NO macOS and NO Windows runner in the schema set. Because the repository is public, a pull request from a fork must never run on fleet hardware: every fleet job keeps (or gains) the fork guard `if: github.event_name != 'pull_request' || github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository`; a fork's pull request therefore gets no CI run, and a maintainer runs it by workflow_dispatch after reading the diff (say this in a comment once at the top of each workflow you touch; never route forks to a hosted runner). `pull_request_target` workflows (cla.yml) run the BASE branch's code and never check out the pull request's head; they may run on the fleet as long as no step checks out or executes the head. The toolchains (go, jdk 21, elixir/OTP, dart, node with TypeScript 5.9.3, dotnet, rust, lua 5.4.8, luau, staticcheck 2026.2.2, nilaway, gcc/g++/clang, make, cmake) are on the runner's PATH from ~/sdk and the bench OS packages. internal/ci (read ci_test.go: `workflows(t, root)` reads every workflow, keyed by base name) holds the repository's workflow tests; .github/actionlint.yaml lists the self-hosted labels actionlint accepts and still names only the retired `studio` and `space` labels. Go: export GOFLAGS=-mod=readonly NOVA_TEST_NO_HOST=1; every `go test` gets `-timeout 600s`; every new test opens with `t.Parallel()`. Build and test only on the Linux bench JOB.md names, never the working Mac.

This card adds the lint and moves the small workflows. (a) The lint: internal/ci/fleet_runners_test.go reads every file under .github/workflows and fails, naming file:line, workflow and job, for any runs-on value, matrix os/runner value or expression branch that names a GitHub-hosted label (ubuntu-*, macos-*, windows-*), and for any `run:` line that installs a tool globally (the list in THE RULE), unless the workflow+job pair is listed in the test file's own exceptions table (a Go slice in fleet_runners_test.go, so every exception is reviewed as code) with a reason. The table has two kinds of rows: DISABLED rows (the job carries `if: false` and a `# DISABLED:` comment; the lint checks both are present), and TEMPORARY rows naming the card that removes them; this card adds TEMPORARY rows for every job in ci-full.yml (removed by schema-shared-ci-full-on-fleet) and in certify.yml (removed by schema-shared-certify-on-fleet), and no other temporary rows. A second test plants a hosted label and a global install into a scratch copy of one workflow under t.TempDir() and asserts the lint names both (the negative control; register it in make/negative-controls.json as that file's convention says). (b) The moves: ci-fast.yml's four jobs drop the `|| '[\"ubuntu-latest\"]'` below-main fallback and run on `[self-hosted, Linux, X64, fleet, schema]` for every event (the paragraph that explains the fallback is rewritten to say below-main pull requests run on the fleet; keep the leg matrix's own skip-below-main rule unless the measured fleet load allows it, and say which in the report); cla.yml, caa-self-test.yml, leg.yml, promotion-tag.yml, release.yml and schema-engine-is-the-oracle.yml move to the fleet labels with the fork guard; any global install in them becomes a presence check. .github/actionlint.yaml lists `fleet`, `schema` and `slow` and drops `studio` and `space`. Existing internal/ci tests that pin a workflow's text are updated only as far as the move needs.

