# Resource bounds: the documented factor, the depth cap and the engine reference (shared port of schema)

Glenn, 2026-10-18: denial-of-service hardening for every port. THIS CARD DEFINES the bounds all ports are held to, and holds the Go engine and Go generated readers to them first. Decide and document in docs/SPEC-TABLES.md (normative, with row ids when schema-spec-pin-design-row-ids has landed; else plain MUST sentences): the allocation factor (bytes allocated by a read are at most input size times F, state F and why), the nesting depth cap and the refusal name for exceeding it, and the shape of a hostile resource test (huge counts, deep nesting, recursion, giant strings, many unknown fields). Add the refusal name to the engine and to the generator contract. Implement and test the bounds in the Go engine and the Go generated readers with an allocation counter (testing.AllocsPerRun or runtime.MemStats, with no real time). Then the 12 per-port cards (schema-<port>-resource-bounds) apply it in each language.

Files you may touch: the PATHS above, plus the NEW files the card needs (the card's own make file make/schema-shared-resource-bounds-every-port.mk (the Makefile includes make/*.mk, so no Makefile edit is needed), its own workflow .github/workflows/schema-shared-resource-bounds-every-port.yml (a schedule and workflow_dispatch trigger for nightly parts, push or pull_request for fast parts), and test files; cards share no workflow or Makefile edits so they land independently. When the work needs a file outside PATHS (the port's test tree, generator, fixtures, saved corpus), report PATHS-PROPOSED with the paths and stop that part as not-done). Do not add needs on other cards: this lens stands alone. Worktree: the staged checkout JOB.md names; branch: the card's own; base: sprint/schema-shared.
Libraries considered: the Go standard library and the repository's own tools (tools/negativecontrols, tools/slowgatescan, internal/ci) for gates and tests; the checker or fuzzer named above for the analysis itself, installed fleet-wide from its official source; no hand-written reimplementation of any checker.

